self_contained_problem:
Background and setup. Consider disordered statistical systems whose effective low-energy behavior is dominated by strong, frozen-in randomness. Two complementary renormalization-group (RG) philosophies have been used to study such systems:

(a) Strong-disorder RG (SDRG): one iteratively decimates the strongest local couplings/fields, tracking only the growing tails (rare events) of certain disorder distributions. This approach has been carried out successfully for a small number of special models (e.g. random transverse-field Ising chains, certain random spin chains, random walks/diffusion in random media, and a few related problems), where the flow drives the system to an "infinite-disorder" fixed point characterized by exponentially broad distributions.

(b) Functional renormalization group (FRG): one writes a flow equation for an entire functional (typically a renormalized disorder correlator R(u) or, more generally, the joint probability distribution P[\,\cdot\,] of a disorder field), keeping the full functional dependence rather than only a few moments.

Problem. Construct a single functional renormalization-group framework that simultaneously:

1. Applies to strong-disorder systems whose fixed points are of infinite-disorder type (so that typical and rare events are governed by very different scales);
2. Flows the full probability distribution P_\ell of the relevant disorder degree(s) of freedom under coarse-graining at scale \ell, rather than only the rapidly diverging moments or the extreme tails associated with rare events, so that statistics of typical events are captured;
3. Reduces, in appropriate limits, to the established SDRG decimation rules on the known solvable strong-disorder models (such as random transverse-field Ising chains, random Heisenberg/XX chains, Sinai diffusion, and related cases);
4. Extends beyond this short list of solved examples, providing controlled flow equations and computable fixed-point distributions for a broader class of strong-disorder problems (for instance higher-dimensional random quantum magnets, random elastic manifolds in the strong-pinning regime, or generic systems with broadly distributed couplings).

Question. Does such a unified FRG framework for strong-disorder systems exist, and if so, exhibit it explicitly: give the flow equation acting on the full distribution, identify its infinite-disorder fixed points, and demonstrate consistency with the known SDRG results on the solvable benchmark models while producing new predictions for at least one model outside that list.

A supernova (SN) is the explosive endpoint of certain stars, observable as a transient luminous event whose properties (light curves, spectra, ejecta composition, energetics, progenitor channel, and rates) depend on physical conditions of the progenitor system and its host environment. Consider the population of supernovae across cosmic history, parameterized by:

(i) metallicity Z of the progenitor and its environment;
(ii) stellar population (e.g., age distribution, initial mass function, binarity fraction) from which progenitors are drawn;
(iii) cosmic age / redshift z, i.e., the epoch in the history of the universe at which the explosion occurs;
(iv) host galaxy environment (galaxy type, local stellar density, gas content, star-formation rate, ISM properties).

Characterize quantitatively how each observable class of supernovae (e.g., Type Ia, Type II, stripped-envelope core-collapse types Ib/Ic, superluminous supernovae, etc.) and their physical properties — including explosion rates per unit stellar mass or star formation, peak luminosities, ejecta masses, nucleosynthetic yields, progenitor masses, and standardizable distance-indicator relations — vary as functions of (Z, stellar population, cosmic age, environment) across cosmic time. Furthermore, determine how these dependencies must be incorporated as systematic corrections, priors, or selection effects when supernovae are used as probes of cosmology, galactic chemical evolution, and large-scale structure of the universe.

Let H be a hypothesis class indexed by a parameter θ ranging over a measurable space Θ. A prior on H is a (σ-finite) measure π on Θ; π is called proper if π(Θ) < ∞ (and may then be normalized to a probability measure), and improper otherwise. A prior assignment rule is said to satisfy the symmetry principle when, for any finite Θ, it assigns equal mass to every θ ∈ Θ (the uniform distribution). For "compact" smooth parametric families, Jeffreys' prior π_J(θ) ∝ √det I(θ), where I(θ) is the Fisher information matrix, provides a canonical reparametrization-invariant extension of this symmetry principle, but on non-compact parameter spaces such as Θ = ℕ or Θ = ℝ it generally yields an improper prior (e.g. Lebesgue measure on ℝ, or counting measure on ℕ), which is often unacceptable for inductive inference because it cannot be normalized and may fail to yield well-defined posterior predictive probabilities.

Problem. Formulate a general principle for assigning a proper prior π on an arbitrary (possibly countably infinite or non-compact continuous) parameter space Θ that:
(i) reduces to the uniform distribution when Θ is finite;
(ii) is invariant under smooth reparametrizations of Θ (i.e. transforms as a measure under bijective sufficiently regular changes of coordinates);
(iii) is proper, i.e. π(Θ) < ∞, on classes such as Θ = ℕ and Θ = ℝ;
(iv) extends or agrees with Jeffreys' prior in the regular compact parametric case.

Either exhibit such a rule together with a characterization theorem, or prove that no rule simultaneously satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

Consider the following classes of finitely presented groups, arising from fundamental groups of complements of codimension-2 embeddings:
- K_0 = the class consisting of the infinite cyclic group Z (groups of complements of S^0 in S^2);
- K_1 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 1-spheres in S^3 (classical knot groups);
- K_2 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 2-spheres in S^4 (2-knot groups);
- K_3 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 3-spheres in S^5 (equivalently, finitely presented groups G with H_1(G) ≅ Z, H_2(G) = 0, and admitting an element whose normal closure is G);
- S = the class of groups of complements of orientable closed surfaces smoothly embedded in S^4;
- M = the class of groups of complements of smooth embeddings into simply connected closed 4-manifolds;
- G = the class of all finitely presented groups.

These satisfy K_0 ⊊ K_1 ⊊ K_2 ⊊ K_3 ⊊ S ⊊ M ⊊ G.

It is known that whenever A, B ∈ {K_0, K_1, K_2, K_3, S, M, G} with B ⊊ A and K_3 ⊆ A, there is no algorithm that, given a finite presentation of a group G known to lie in A, decides whether G ∈ B.

Question: Does the same undecidability statement hold when A = K_2? That is, for every B ∈ {K_0, K_1} (the proper subclasses of K_2 in the list), is it true that there is no algorithm that, given a finite presentation of a 2-knot group, decides membership in B?

Equivalently (as a sufficient condition): does there exist a 2-knot group (a member of K_2) with unsolvable word problem?

Let $k$ be a field and let $n>2$ be an integer. Consider the polynomial ring $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ and its automorphism group $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$, consisting of $k$-algebra automorphisms of $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ (equivalently, polynomial automorphisms of affine $n$-space $\mathbb{A}^n_k$). Inside $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$ define:
\begin{itemize}
\item the affine subgroup $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$, consisting of automorphisms $(x_1,\dots,x_n)\mapsto (L_1,\dots,L_n)$ where each $L_i$ is a polynomial of total degree $\le 1$;
\item the triangular (de Jonquières) subgroup $\mathrm{BA}_n(k)$, consisting of automorphisms of the form $x_i\mapsto a_i x_i + f_i(x_{i+1},\dots,x_n)$ with $a_i\in k^\times$ and $f_i\in k[x_{i+1},\dots,x_n]$;
\item the tame subgroup $\mathrm{TA}_n(k)=\langle \mathrm{Aff}_n(k),\mathrm{BA}_n(k)\rangle$.
\end{itemize}
Determine whether $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$ is a maximal subgroup of $\mathrm{TA}_n(k)$; that is, whether there exists no subgroup $H$ with $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)\subsetneq H\subsetneq \mathrm{TA}_n(k)$.

For Population II giant stars (low-metallicity, low-mass evolved stars), develop a physically grounded and empirically calibrated description of the mass loss occurring during the Red Giant Branch (RGB) and Asymptotic Giant Branch (AGB) evolutionary phases. Specifically, determine:

(a) the characteristic timescales over which mass loss operates during the RGB and AGB stages;
(b) the underlying physical mechanisms that drive the mass loss (e.g., radiation pressure on dust, pulsation-driven winds, chromospheric activity, Alfvén waves, or other processes), and the conditions under which each mechanism dominates;
(c) a quantitative law expressing the instantaneous mass-loss rate \dot{M} as a function of fundamental stellar parameters — luminosity L, effective temperature T_{eff}, surface gravity g (equivalently mass M and radius R), and metallicity Z — that is justified from first principles rather than extrapolated from empirical fits calibrated on Population I bright giants (such as the Reimers prescription \dot{M} = -\eta\, L R / M with a free parameter \eta).

The goal is a prescription \dot{M}(L, T_{eff}, M, R, Z, t) suitable for direct incorporation into stellar evolution codes that (i) reproduces empirical constraints on Population II giants (e.g., horizontal-branch morphology in globular clusters, white-dwarf masses, AGB tip luminosities), (ii) reduces or removes the dependence on tunable efficiency parameters, and (iii) provides reliable extrapolation to metallicity and stellar-parameter regimes not covered by Population I calibrations.

Consider the Hamiltonian formulation of General Relativity in the extended phase space approach. In this approach, one starts from the Einstein–Hilbert action (possibly with matter), introduces gauge-fixing conditions through additional variables together with the corresponding ghost variables, and constructs an effective Lagrangian whose Hamiltonian formulation lives in an extended phase space that includes the gauge and ghost sectors alongside the original gravitational variables and their conjugate momenta. Within this framework one obtains:

(i) a Schrödinger-type equation for the wave function of the Universe that depends explicitly on the chosen reference frame (gauge);
(ii) a unitary evolution of the gravitating system in the extended phase space;
(iii) a consistent treatment of constraints without imposing them as operator restrictions on physical states (in contrast with Dirac quantization and the Wheeler–DeWitt equation);
(iv) the equivalence, at the classical level, with the standard Hamiltonian formulation of General Relativity after the gauge-fixing conditions are imposed.

These results have been established for cosmological and other minisuperspace-type models, i.e. for systems possessing only finitely many degrees of freedom obtained from General Relativity by symmetry reduction.

Problem. Extend the construction and the four properties (i)–(iv) above to full (3+1)-dimensional General Relativity, regarded as a field theory with infinitely many degrees of freedom. Concretely, build an extended phase space quantization of the full gravitational field, including a precise definition of the path integral measure, the associated ghost sector, and the gauge-dependent Hamiltonian; and prove that the resulting theory satisfies the analogues of (i)–(iv), in particular yielding a gauge-dependent Schrödinger equation for the wave functional of the gravitational field together with unitary evolution in the extended phase space.

Let H be a rotationally scalar (i.e., SO(3)-invariant) many-body Hamiltonian acting on the antisymmetric Fock space of A nucleons. Consider the variational problem of minimizing the energy E[D] = Tr(HD) over many-body density operators D that are themselves rotational scalars (i.e., commute with the total angular momentum operators). For any such scalar D, the associated one-body density ρ(r⃗) = ⟨r⃗|ρ̂|r⃗⟩ depends only on r = |r⃗|, so it reduces to a monopole radial profile ρ₀(r), r ∈ [0, ∞), with ∫₀^∞ ρ₀(r) 4πr² dr = A. By a constrained Hohenberg–Kohn-type reduction, the minimization yields a scalar energy functional F₀[ρ₀] of the radial profile alone.

Formulate precise mathematical criteria, expressed solely in terms of features of the radial profile ρ₀ (such as its slope ρ₀'(R) at the nuclear surface radius R, higher derivatives at the surface, or radial moments ⟨rⁿ⟩ = ∫₀^∞ rⁿ ρ₀(r) 4πr² dr / A, n ∈ ℝ), that distinguish among the following classes of nuclei:

(a) spherical versus (axially or otherwise) deformed nuclei;
(b) "hard" (rigid surface) versus "soft" (fluctuating surface) nuclei;
(c) triaxial nuclei;
(d) halo nuclei.

In particular, prove or disprove the conjecture that the magnitude of the surface slope |ρ₀'(R)| is strictly smaller for deformed nuclei than for spherical ones with comparable mass and charge, and identify the minimal set of functionals of ρ₀ whose values determine membership in each of the classes (a)–(d).

Consider a finite harmonic-oscillator shell-model setting in which neutron and proton one-body densities $\rho_n$ and $\rho_p$ are expanded in a finite basis and represented by finite vectors of density-polynomial coefficients $(C_{n,1},\dots,C_{n,N_n})$ and $(C_{p,1},\dots,C_{p,N_p})$. For a given many-body Hamiltonian, write the energy of a configuration-mixing trial state as a polynomial in the mixing coefficients, and impose the Levy–Lieb density constraints (which fix the densities $\rho_n$ and $\rho_p$ in terms of the mixing coefficients) together with the normalization constraint as algebraic polynomial equations in those mixing coefficients. After eliminating the constrained mixing variables and the surviving coefficients via polynomial elimination, one obtains an implicit polynomial relation
$$R(\eta, C_{n,1},\dots,C_{n,N_n}, C_{p,1},\dots,C_{p,N_p}) \;=\; 0$$
that links the minimal energy $\eta$ (as a function of the density) to the density parameters, thereby defining an implicit polynomial density functional.

Develop and justify rigorous algebraic strategies that:
(i) practically handle the very large multivariate polynomials $R$ produced by elimination when the number of parameters is large;
(ii) systematically compare two such implicit relations $R$ and $R'$ obtained from two different finite-basis choices, and quantify in what sense they define the same (or different) density functional;
(iii) provide controlled extrapolation procedures for $\eta$ as a function of the density parameters when direct elimination becomes computationally infeasible, with rigorous error or convergence guarantees;
(iv) embed the resulting rigorous polynomial density functionals into larger nuclear many-body models in a usable way.

Let $p>5$ be a prime and let $\left(\tfrac{\cdot}{p}\right)$ denote the Legendre symbol. Define the polynomial sequence
$$T_n := \sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k}\qquad(n\ge 0).$$
Prove or disprove the following statements.

(1) One has the congruence
$$\sum_{n=0}^{p-1}\frac{357n+103}{2160^{n}}\binom{2n}{n}T_n\;\equiv\; p\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)\!\left(54+49\left(\tfrac{105}{p}\right)\right)\pmod{p^2}.$$

(2) One has
$$\sum_{n=0}^{p-1}\frac{\binom{2n}{n}}{2160^{n}}T_n\;\equiv\; R(p)\pmod{p^2},$$
where $R(p)$ is determined by the residues of $p$ as follows (with $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$ in each case):

• $4x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$ and $p=x^2+105y^2$;
• $2x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$, and $2p=x^2+105y^2$;
• $2p-12x^2$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $p=3x^2+35y^2$;
• $2p-6x^2$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$, and $2p=3x^2+35y^2$;
• $20x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$, and $p=5x^2+21y^2$;
• $10x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$, and $2p=5x^2+21y^2$;
• $28x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$, and $p=7x^2+15y^2$;
• $14x^2-2p$ if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$, and $2p=7x^2+15y^2$;
• $0$ if $\left(\tfrac{-105}{p}\right)=-1$.

(3) The series identity
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{357n+103}{2160^{n}}\binom{2n}{n}T_n=\frac{90}{\pi}$$
holds.

Let $d \ge 1$ be an integer (the number of stations). Let $G$ be a $d \times d$ real matrix (the routing-information matrix), let $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a fixed vector (the vector of netflow rates), and let $\Gamma$ be a $d \times d$ symmetric positive semidefinite matrix (the covariance matrix). Set $R := I + G$, where $I$ is the $d \times d$ identity, and let $\xi^*=(\xi^*(t))_{t\ge 0}$ be a $d$-dimensional Brownian motion with drift $-\theta$ and covariance matrix $\Gamma$, started at the origin, defined on a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions.

Determine, for arbitrary such data $(\theta,\Gamma,G)$, whether there exists a pair of continuous adapted processes $(W^*,Y^*)$ taking values in $\mathbb{R}^d$ such that, almost surely:
(i) $W^*(t)\ge 0$ componentwise for all $t\ge 0$;
(ii) $Y^*(0)=0$ and each component $Y^*_j$ is nondecreasing;
(iii) $W^*(t)=\xi^*(t)-G\,W^*(t)+Y^*(t)$ for all $t\ge 0$, equivalently $R\,W^*(t)=\xi^*(t)+Y^*(t)$;
(iv) for each $j=1,\dots,d$, $\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{W^*_j(s)>0\}}\,dY^*_j(s)=0$, i.e.\ $Y^*_j$ may increase only at times $t$ when $W^*_j(t)=0$.

Moreover, if such $(W^*,Y^*)$ exists, decide whether it is pathwise unique. State precise conditions on $(\theta,\Gamma,G)$ that are necessary and sufficient for existence and uniqueness.

Consider a class N of synchronous finite-state neuronal networks defined as follows. A network in N consists of n nodes (neurons), each carrying a state in a finite set S (for example, S = {0, 1, ..., R} where R encodes a refractory period, with state 0 representing rest and state 1 representing firing). The network is endowed with a directed graph D on the n nodes whose edges carry signed weights (excitatory or inhibitory). At each discrete time step, all neurons update their states synchronously according to a fixed deterministic rule that depends on the weighted sum of the current states of the in-neighbors of each neuron and a firing threshold; after firing, a neuron progresses through the refractory states before returning to rest.

For a network in N drawn at random by sampling its underlying directed connectivity D from a prescribed random digraph model (with edge weights chosen by an independent fixed mechanism), study the typical asymptotic and transient dynamics of the resulting deterministic discrete-time dynamical system on S^n. In particular, characterize:
  (a) the attractor structure (number, sizes, and periods of attracting cycles, basin sizes);
  (b) transient behavior (distribution of transient lengths before reaching an attractor);
  (c) phase transitions in the above quantities as parameters of the random connectivity model vary.

Carry out this analysis for each of the following random digraph models in place of the (single-type) Erdős–Rényi digraph G(n, p):
  1. multitype Erdős–Rényi random digraphs, where the n nodes are partitioned into finitely many types and the probability of a directed edge from a node of type i to a node of type j is a prescribed parameter p_{ij};
  2. generic scale-free random digraphs, in which the in- and/or out-degree distributions follow a power law;
  3. small-world random digraphs (for instance, of Watts–Strogatz type), obtained by random rewiring of a regular directed graph.

Determine, for each model, the typical attractor behavior, transient behavior, and phase transitions of the network dynamics in the large-n limit.

Consider the following discrete-time activation process on a random directed graph. Let $n \ge 1$ and form a random digraph $D(n,\pi)$ on vertex set $\{1,\dots,n\}$ by independently including each ordered pair $(i,j)$ with $i \ne j$ as a directed edge with probability $\pi = \pi(n) = c/n$, where $c>0$ is a fixed constant. Each vertex $i$ has a threshold $\mathrm{th}_i = 1$ and an initial activation parameter $p_i = 1$, interpreted as follows: at time $t=0$ a single vertex chosen uniformly at random is active, and at each subsequent step every inactive vertex $i$ becomes active as soon as it has received at least $\mathrm{th}_i = 1$ directed edge from a currently active vertex. Let $A_n(c)$ denote the (random) total number of vertices that are eventually active.

It is known that there exists a critical constant $c_{\mathrm{crit}} \in [1,2]$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $c < c_{\mathrm{crit}}$, then $A_n(c)/n \to 0$ in probability as $n\to\infty$;
\item if $c > c_{\mathrm{crit}}$, then $\liminf_{n\to\infty} \mathbb{E}[A_n(c)]/n > 0$.
\end{itemize}

Prove that $c_{\mathrm{crit}} = 1$.

Consider a system of A interacting nucleons described by a many-body Hamiltonian H including a pairing interaction, and suppose its low-energy large-amplitude collective dynamics is parametrized by a one-dimensional collective coordinate q labeling a smooth path |Φ(q)⟩ of generalized Slater determinants (or HFB quasiparticle vacua) in the many-body Hilbert space. The associated collective Hamiltonian takes the form

   H_coll(q,p) = (1/2) M(q) p^2 + V(q),

where p is the momentum conjugate to q and M(q) is the collective inertia (collective mass). Along the path, single-particle (or quasiparticle) levels near the Fermi surface may cross repeatedly as q varies, pairing correlations evolve, nucleon pairs may "hop" between levels, and the single-particle wave functions change smoothly with q.

Problem. Derive, from the underlying microscopic many-body Hamiltonian H, an explicit and quantitatively predictive expression for the collective mass M(q) along such a large-amplitude collective path, and identify and quantify the separate contributions to M(q) coming from:
  (i) pairing correlations and their q-dependence;
  (ii) repeated level crossings of single-particle/quasiparticle states near the Fermi surface;
  (iii) nucleon-pair hopping (transfer of correlated pairs between single-particle orbitals as q evolves);
  (iv) the smooth q-dependence of single-particle wave functions.

In particular, determine how these four effects combine to set the inertia against changing q, and clarify under what conditions the resulting microscopic M(q) reduces to, or differs significantly from, the standard cranking (Inglis–Belyaev) and adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) expressions.

Consider a many-fermion quantum system (a nucleus) governed by a microscopic Hamiltonian H acting on the antisymmetric Fock space. A microscopic theory of large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) seeks a low-dimensional collective submanifold M embedded in the full TDHF (time-dependent Hartree–Fock) or TDHFB (Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov) phase space of Slater determinants (or generalized product states) such that:

(i) M is approximately invariant under the dynamics generated by H;
(ii) the dynamics restricted to M decouples maximally from non-collective intrinsic degrees of freedom;
(iii) M is determined self-consistently from H (e.g., via the self-consistent collective coordinate method, the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method, or equivalent invariance-principle formulations of the TDHF/TDHFB equations), without prescribing collective coordinates by hand.

Existing constructions of such submanifolds and the resulting collective Hamiltonian are formulated in the laboratory frame (or in a frame with vanishing or small angular velocity) and are valid only in the low-spin regime, where the modification of the intrinsic mean field induced by rotation can be neglected.

Problem. Construct a microscopic theory of LACM applicable to high-spin states that satisfies all of the following:

(a) it is formulated in a rotating intrinsic frame so that the angular velocity ω (or, equivalently, the total angular momentum I) appears as a dynamical variable on equal footing with the LACM coordinates;

(b) the collective submanifold M(ω) and the associated collective inertia and potential are determined self-consistently together with the cranked mean field, so that variations of the intrinsic single-particle structure caused by rapid rotation (e.g., Coriolis antipairing, alignment of high-j quasiparticles, shape changes with spin) are included nonperturbatively rather than treated as external perturbations on a low-spin solution;

(c) it reduces, in the limit ω → 0, to a known self-consistent microscopic LACM theory at low spin;

(d) after requantization on M(ω), it yields a collective Schrödinger-type equation describing coupled large-amplitude shape (and pairing) dynamics with rotation, capable of describing phenomena such as band crossings, shape transitions along the yrast line, and rotation-induced changes of the collective potential and mass parameters.

Provide such a construction, or prove that no construction satisfying (a)–(d) exists within the TDHF/TDHFB framework.

In the microscopic theory of nuclear many-body dynamics, large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) refers to slow, large-amplitude time evolution of a self-bound fermionic many-body system that can be described, in even-even nuclei, by a small number of collective coordinates parametrizing a manifold of generalized Slater determinants (e.g., via the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method, the constrained Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov plus adiabatic time-dependent HFB approach, or the generator-coordinate method). For systems built on a quasiparticle vacuum these methods provide non-perturbative collective Hamiltonians coupling shape and pairing degrees of freedom.

For odd-mass nuclei, and more generally for fermionic many-body states containing one or more unpaired quasiparticles on top of a deforming quasiparticle vacuum, the existing non-perturbative LACM frameworks do not directly apply, because the presence of blocked quasiparticle states breaks the simple structure of the collective manifold and couples single-particle motion of the odd quasiparticle(s) to the collective shape/pairing dynamics in an essential way. The standard alternative, particle–vibration coupling, is intrinsically a small-amplitude (perturbative) expansion around a static mean field.

Problem. Construct a microscopic, fully non-perturbative theoretical framework that describes large-amplitude collective motion in odd-A nuclei, and more generally in self-consistent mean-field systems with several blocked quasiparticles, in such a way that:
(i) it does not rely on a small-amplitude or perturbative particle–vibration-coupling expansion;
(ii) it treats the coupling between the intrinsic motion of the unpaired quasiparticle(s) and the collective (shape and pairing) degrees of freedom on the same footing, yielding a unified set of collective coordinates, momenta, and a collective Hamiltonian;
(iii) it reduces, in the limit of an even-even quasiparticle vacuum, to an established non-perturbative LACM theory (such as the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method or adiabatic TDHFB);
(iv) it provides computable equations (e.g., self-consistent equations determining the collective submanifold and inertial functions) suitable for application to realistic effective interactions or energy density functionals.

Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are brief, intense flashes of high-energy electromagnetic radiation produced by relativistic jets launched from compact astrophysical sources. Their "prompt emission" phase exhibits a broadband spectrum, typically modeled with a dominant nonthermal component, and possibly an additional thermal (blackbody-like) component associated with the jet photosphere (the surface where the outflow becomes optically thin to its own radiation).

Identify the radiative mechanism responsible for the nonthermal component of the GRB prompt high-energy emission. Specifically, determine which of the following processes produces it:
(a) optically thin synchrotron radiation from relativistic electrons gyrating in an ordered or large-scale magnetic field;
(b) jitter radiation from relativistic electrons traversing small-scale, randomly oriented magnetic field fluctuations whose coherence length is much smaller than the electron Larmor radius;
(c) synchrotron self-Compton (SSC) emission, in which synchrotron photons are inverse-Compton scattered by the same population of relativistic electrons.

In addition, determine whether the prompt emission spectrum also contains a distinct photospheric thermal component arising from radiation released at the jet photosphere, and, if so, characterize when and how it coexists with the nonthermal component.

Consider an idealized model of infinite "α matter": an infinite, homogeneous system of nucleons (protons and neutrons in equal numbers, i.e. symmetric nuclear matter) at low baryon density, in which the nucleons are clustered into freely moving α particles (4-nucleon bound states with the quantum numbers of ⁴He). Treat the system fully microscopically, starting from realistic nucleon–nucleon (and, if desired, three-nucleon) interactions, and require that the many-nucleon wave function be fully antisymmetric under exchange of any two nucleons (so that antisymmetrization between nucleons belonging to different α clusters is properly included).

Carry out a first-principles many-body calculation of this system and determine, as functions of the nucleon number density ρ in the low-density regime:

(a) the ground-state energy per nucleon E(ρ)/A of symmetric matter built from α clusters;

(b) the α–α pair distribution function g_{αα}(r), defined so that ρ_α g_{αα}(r) is the conditional density of α-particle centers of mass at separation r given an α at the origin, where ρ_α = ρ/4;

(c) the in-medium α-particle elastic form factor F_α(q;ρ), i.e. the Fourier transform of the intrinsic four-nucleon density distribution of an α cluster embedded in the medium, and in particular its modification relative to the free-space ⁴He form factor as ρ increases.

Provide a calculational scheme that yields these three quantities consistently within a single antisymmetrized many-body framework.

Consider the low-energy interaction of an antikaon ($K^-$) with nucleons. Define the complex $K^-p$ scattering length $a_{K^-p}=\Re a_{K^-p}+i\,\Im a_{K^-p}$ (in fm), the corresponding $K^-n$ scattering length $a_{K^-n}$, and the isospin-decomposed antikaon–nucleon scattering lengths $a_0$ and $a_1$ (in the $I=0$ and $I=1$ channels), related by $a_{K^-p}=\tfrac12(a_0+a_1)$ and $a_{K^-n}=a_1$. Let $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$ denote the coupled-channel $\bar K N\to\bar K N$ scattering amplitudes (with channels $\bar K N$, $\pi\Sigma$, $\pi\Lambda$, $\eta\Lambda$, $\eta\Sigma$, $K\Xi$) for center-of-mass energies $\sqrt{s}$ in a subthreshold region (down to roughly $35\,$MeV below the $\bar K N$ threshold), and let the energy shift $\Delta E_{1s}$ and width $\Gamma_{1s}$ of the $1s$ state of a kaonic atom be related to the corresponding kaonic-atom scattering length through a Deser-type formula including isospin-breaking and recoil corrections.

Determine values of $\Re a_{K^-p}$, $\Im a_{K^-p}$, $a_{K^-n}$, and $a_0,a_1$, together with the subthreshold $\bar K N$ amplitudes $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$, that are simultaneously consistent with:
(i) the measured $1s$ energy shift and width of kaonic hydrogen;
(ii) the measured $1s$ shift and width of kaonic deuterium (after solving the $K^-pn$ three-body problem with full coupled-channel dynamics);
(iii) low-energy $K^-p\to K^-p,\bar K^0 n,\pi^\pm\Sigma^\mp,\pi^0\Sigma^0,\pi^0\Lambda$ cross-section data;
(iv) the measured $\pi\Sigma$ invariant-mass spectra in the $\Lambda(1405)$ region.

Specify how the above empirical inputs constrain the analytic continuation of $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$ to the subthreshold region used as input in calculations of kaonic nuclei (e.g.\ kaonic $^3$He, $^4$He, and putative deeply-bound $\bar K$-nuclear states).

Several stopped-kaon and antiproton-annihilation experiments (e.g., E549, FINUDA, OBELIX, FOPI, and earlier K⁻d bubble-chamber measurements) have reported peak-like structures in invariant-mass spectra of final states such as Λp and ppπ⁻. A "Bound Kaonic System" (BKS) is a hypothetical strongly bound nuclear state involving an antikaon (K̄) and one or more nucleons (e.g., K⁻pp), whose two-body or multi-body decay would produce a peak in invariant-mass distributions of the corresponding decay products at an energy below the K̄ + nucleons threshold.

Question: For each reported peak in Λp, ppπ⁻, and related invariant-mass spectra obtained from these K⁻-induced and p̄-induced reactions on light nuclei, determine whether the structure constitutes genuine evidence for a bound kaonic system, or whether it can be quantitatively reproduced by conventional reaction mechanisms, including:
(i) threshold cusp effects associated with the opening of nearby channels (e.g., the Σ⁰nπ⁻ or ΣN thresholds);
(ii) two-nucleon absorption processes K⁻NN → YN with subsequent final-state interactions;
(iii) ΣN → ΛN conversion in the nuclear medium;
(iv) other final-state interaction effects.

Provide a unified interpretation that simultaneously accounts for all such observations across the listed experiments and reconciles the existing disagreements among them.

Consider a coherent polycrystalline solid in which the local crystallographic state is described by two coupled fields: a positional/density order parameter ψ(x,t) encoding the periodic crystalline pattern, and an orientation field θ(x,t) (taking values in SO(2) in two dimensions, or SO(3) in three dimensions) representing the local rotation of the crystal lattice. In a coherent crystal, the lattice is connected throughout the body, so spatial gradients of θ correspond to geometrically necessary dislocations, whose Nye dislocation density tensor α can be expressed in terms of derivatives of θ and of the elastic distortion.

Standard phase-field models of polycrystalline solidification postulate a purely local relaxational dynamics for the orientation field, schematically of the form
   ∂θ/∂t = − M_θ · δF/δθ,
where F is a free-energy functional and M_θ is a (possibly orientation- and ψ-dependent) mobility. Such an equation does not, in general, respect the kinematic constraint that, in a coherent crystal, changes of θ are produced by the motion of dislocations and must be consistent with the evolution of the dislocation density α, with elastic interactions among dislocations, with externally applied strain, and with interactions with other defects (grain boundaries, free surfaces, second-phase particles, etc.).

Problem. Formulate a tractable, physically consistent evolution equation for the orientation field θ(x,t) in a coherent polycrystalline body, compatible with a phase-field (or phase-field-crystal) description of the underlying density field, such that:
 (i) ∂θ/∂t is derived from, and consistent with, the kinematic evolution of the Nye dislocation density tensor α[θ, u] (with u the elastic displacement);
 (ii) the resulting dynamics correctly accounts for the elastic interactions between dislocations and for an applied external strain field;
 (iii) it incorporates the interaction of dislocations with other defects present in the system;
 (iv) it reduces to known limits (e.g. grain-boundary motion by coupled shear/migration, recovery, polygonization) in appropriate regimes;
 (v) it is computationally tractable at the mesoscale, in the sense that it can be coupled to standard phase-field equations for the density and concentration fields without prohibitive cost.

Provide such an equation, or prove that, under stated physical hypotheses, no equation satisfying (i)–(v) exists.

For a field $F$, let $\mathrm{Br.dim}(F)$ denote the Brauer dimension of $F$, defined as the smallest non-negative integer $n$ such that for every central simple $F$-algebra $A$ one has $\mathrm{ind}(A) \mid \mathrm{per}(A)^{n}$, where $\mathrm{per}(A)$ is the order of the class of $A$ in the Brauer group $\mathrm{Br}(F)$ and $\mathrm{ind}(A)$ is the degree of a minimal splitting field; set $\mathrm{Br.dim}(F)=\infty$ if no such $n$ exists. Let $F(t)$ denote the field of rational functions in one variable over $F$.

Define a notion of "dimension" $\dim$, assigning a value $\dim F \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}$ to each field $F$ in some sufficiently large and useful class of fields $\mathcal{C}$ (closed under the operation $F \mapsto F(t)$), such that the following two properties hold for every $F \in \mathcal{C}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathrm{Br.dim}(F) \leq \dim F - 1$;
\item $\dim F(t) = \dim F + 1$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $F$ be a field and let $H = C_3 \times C_3$ be embedded as a transitive subgroup of the symmetric group $S_9$ via its regular representation (so that $H$ acts on the set $\{1,2,\dots,9\}$ by left multiplication after a fixed identification of this set with the underlying set of $H$). Let $H$ act on the rational function field $F(x_1,\dots,x_9)$ by permuting the variables according to this embedding $H \hookrightarrow S_9$, and define
$$Z_H(F,9) := F(x_1,\dots,x_9)^{H}.$$
Determine whether the field $Z_H(F,9)$ is:
(a) rational (i.e., purely transcendental) over $F$;
(b) stably rational over $F$ (i.e., $Z_H(F,9)(y_1,\dots,y_m)$ is purely transcendental over $F$ for some $m\ge 0$);
(c) retract rational over $F$ (in the sense of Saltman: $Z_H(F,9)$ is the fraction field of an $F$-algebra $A$ such that the identity map of $A$ factors through a localization of a polynomial $F$-algebra).

Let $F$ be a field and let $\mathrm{Br}(F)$ denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of finite-dimensional central simple $F$-algebras under the tensor product, equivalently $H^2(\mathrm{Gal}(F^{\mathrm{sep}}/F), (F^{\mathrm{sep}})^\times)$. For a prime $p$, let $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ denote the $p$-primary component of $\mathrm{Br}(F)$, that is, the subgroup of elements whose order is a power of $p$. A subgroup $D \subseteq (\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ is called divisible if for every $x \in D$ and every positive integer $n$ there exists $y \in D$ with $ny = x$. An abelian $p$-group is called elementary abelian if every nonzero element has order $p$.

Prove or disprove: for every field $F$ and every prime $p$, at least one of the following three conditions holds:
(a) $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p = 0$;
(b) $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) $p = 2$ and $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_2$ is an elementary abelian $2$-group.

An isothetic (axis-aligned) rectangle in the plane is a closed rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Let $\mathcal{R} = \{R_1, \ldots, R_n\}$ be a finite collection of isothetic rectangles with pairwise disjoint interiors. The rectangle visibility graph of $\mathcal{R}$ is the simple graph $G$ on vertex set $\{R_1, \ldots, R_n\}$ in which two distinct rectangles $R_i$ and $R_j$ are adjacent if and only if there exists an axis-aligned open rectangle $B$ of positive (finite) width such that:
(i) $B$ is either horizontal (its long axis parallel to the $x$-axis) or vertical (its long axis parallel to the $y$-axis);
(ii) $B$ meets $R_i$ and $R_j$ along opposite sides and is otherwise disjoint from $R_i \cup R_j$;
(iii) $B$ has empty intersection with the interior of every other rectangle $R_k \in \mathcal{R}$, $k \ne i, j$.

A simple graph $G$ is called a rectangle visibility graph if it arises as the rectangle visibility graph of some such collection $\mathcal{R}$.

Problem: Give a complete combinatorial characterization of the class of rectangle visibility graphs, i.e., determine necessary and sufficient conditions on a finite simple graph $G$ for $G$ to be a rectangle visibility graph.

Let $(R,\mathfrak{m})$ be a regular local ring of dimension $n$, and let $I \subseteq R$ be an ideal whose big height $\mathrm{bight}(I) = e$ (the maximum of the heights of the associated primes of $I$). For a positive integer $m$, let $I^{(m)}$ denote the $m$-th symbolic power of $I$, i.e., $I^{(m)} = \bigcap_{P \in \mathrm{Ass}(R/I)} (I^m R_P \cap R)$. Determine whether each of the following strengthened symbolic-power containments holds for every positive integer $r$:

(1) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{rn - r} I^{r}$ for all $m \geq rn$;

(2) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{rn - r - (n-1)} I^{r}$ for all $m \geq rn - (n-1)$;

(3) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{re - r} I^{r}$ for all $m \geq re$;

(4) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{re - r - (e-1)} I^{r}$ for all $m \geq re - (e-1)$.

Consider the family of diluted dipolar-coupled Ising magnets obtained by randomly substituting nonmagnetic Y ions for magnetic rare-earth ions in the following crystalline hosts:

(a) LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4, in which Ho^{3+} ions occupy a tetragonal lattice with strong crystal-field anisotropy producing an effective Ising doublet, coupled by long-range magnetic dipole interactions and weaker exchange, with hyperfine coupling to the Ho nuclear spin and an applied transverse magnetic field producing a tunable transverse-field Ising model (TFIM);

(b) the diluted spin-ice pyrochlores (Dy_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and (Ho_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7, in which the magnetic ions occupy a pyrochlore lattice with local <111> Ising anisotropy and dipolar interactions giving rise to the spin-ice ground-state manifold;

(c) the rare-earth hydroxides Ho_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 and Dy_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3, proposed as TFIM-realizing materials with Ising-like crystal-field doublets and dipolar interactions on a hexagonal lattice.

In each case let H = -\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i\ne j} \epsilon_i \epsilon_j J_{ij}^{zz} \sigma_i^z \sigma_j^z - \Gamma \sum_i \epsilon_i \sigma_i^x, where \epsilon_i \in \{0,1\} are i.i.d. Bernoulli(x) site occupations, J_{ij}^{zz} encodes the (anisotropic, long-range) effective Ising couplings appropriate to the host lattice and crystal-field projection, and \Gamma is a transverse field (or effective tunneling generated by hyperfine/CF mixing).

Problem: Determine, for the highly diluted regime x \to 0 of LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4, the nature of the low-temperature collective state (e.g.\ spin-glass, antiglass, ferromagnet, or paramagnet) and identify the mechanism producing the experimentally reported anomalous behavior. In particular, decide whether a comparative analysis — using the dilute pyrochlore Ising magnets (Dy_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and (Ho_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and the dilute hexagonal TFIM candidates Ho_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 and Dy_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 as control systems with differing lattice geometries, single-ion anisotropies, and hyperfine couplings — suffices to isolate which microscopic ingredient (lattice geometry, sign/anisotropy of dipolar couplings, off-diagonal dipolar terms, hyperfine interaction, random-field generation by the transverse field, or rare-earth-specific crystal-field structure) is responsible for the highly dilute behavior of LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4.

Consider the nonequilibrium Green's-function (NEGF) formalism on the Keldysh contour for an interacting fermionic many-body system. Let G(1,2) denote the contour-ordered one-particle Green's function and let Σ[G](1,2) be a self-energy obtained from a Φ-derivable (conserving, in the sense of Baym) many-body approximation, such as second-order Born, GW, or T-matrix. The Kadanoff-Baym equations (KBE) are the integro-differential equations

  (i∂_{t_1} − h(1)) G(1,2) − ∫ Σ[G](1,3) G(3,2) d3 = δ(1,2),

together with their adjoint, where h is the one-body Hamiltonian. Such Φ-derivable approximations preserve the macroscopic conservation laws (particle number, total energy, momentum), but are known to exhibit the following pathologies in time evolution:

  (a) breakdown for sufficiently strong two-body interaction;
  (b) an artificial, correlation-induced damping that, in finite systems, drives the evolution to a non-decaying steady state;
  (c) in extended systems, the appearance of arbitrarily long-lived transient states that ultimately decay to a unique steady state of unclear physical status;
  (d) violation of positivity of two-particle correlation functions (i.e., the two-particle reduced density matrix derived from G can fail to be positive semidefinite).

Problem. Identify and characterize, in a mathematically precise way, the physical regimes (interaction strength, density, dimensionality, finite vs. extended geometry, time scales) in which each of the failures (a)–(d) actually occurs for the KBE with second Born, GW, and T-matrix self-energies, and formulate corrective principles—i.e., systematic modifications of Σ[G] or of the KBE evolution—that simultaneously (i) preserve the macroscopic conservation laws, (ii) eliminate the artificial correlation-induced damping in finite systems, (iii) yield physically justified steady states in extended systems, and (iv) guarantee positivity of all two-particle correlation functions along the evolution.

Consider time-dependent density functional theory (TDDFT) applied to the single-band Hubbard model on a lattice with on-site interaction strength U > 0 and site density n ∈ [0,2]. Within the adiabatic local density approximation (ALDA), the exchange-correlation (xc) potential v_xc(n;U) is taken from the ground-state energy per site of the homogeneous Hubbard model (e.g., from the exact 1D Bethe-ansatz solution or from dynamical mean-field theory in higher dimensions), and the corresponding ALDA xc kernel is defined site-locally and time-locally as

   f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) = ∂ v_xc(n;U) / ∂ n.

This kernel is known to display two qualitative features absent from standard continuum xc kernels:
  (i) sign change: f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) can be either positive or negative depending on (n,U), so it can either reinforce or oppose the bare interaction U in the density-density response;
  (ii) singularity at half filling: for U exceeding the Mott-Hubbard critical value U^{Mott}_c (and for all U > 0 in one dimension), v_xc(n;U) is discontinuous at n = 1, producing a Dirac-delta-like spike in f^{ALDA}_{xc} at n = 1.

Problem. Develop a rigorous TDDFT exchange-correlation kernel f_{xc}(n,n';t-t';U) for the Hubbard model that:
  (a) reduces to f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) in the local adiabatic limit;
  (b) incorporates spatial nonlocality (dependence on sites i ≠ j) and nonadiabatic memory (frequency dependence) in a controlled way;
  (c) preserves the sign-changing behavior in (i) and the singular structure at half filling associated with the Mott-Hubbard metal-insulator transition in (ii) when these features are physically present;
  (d) yields a linear density response function χ(q,ω) consistent with these qualitative features beyond the local adiabatic approximation.

In particular, characterize how the sign-change locus in the (n,U) plane and the half-filling singularity of f_{xc} manifest themselves in the energy/frequency dependence of χ(q,ω), and prove or disprove that these features persist once nonlocal and nonadiabatic corrections are included.

In non-commutative group-based cryptography one designs cryptographic protocols (key-exchange, encryption, digital signatures, etc.) whose security rests on the algorithmic difficulty of certain problems in a non-abelian group G, called the platform group. Typical hard problems considered include the conjugacy search problem (given g, h = x^{-1} g x in G, find a conjugator x), the word problem, the membership problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the decomposition problem, and variants thereof. A platform group G is considered suitable if it satisfies, at least, the following informal requirements:
  (i) elements of G admit a unique, efficiently computable normal form, so that the word problem in G is solvable in (low) polynomial time;
  (ii) the group law and inversion are computable in polynomial time on normal forms;
  (iii) there exist efficient algorithms to sample elements from G according to distributions usable in protocols;
  (iv) the underlying hard problem (e.g. conjugacy search) has no known subexponential-time algorithm on G, and known length-based, linear-algebraic, or quotient attacks fail;
  (v) keys and ciphertexts admit succinct representations (polynomial in the security parameter).

Identify and characterize a class C of finitely generated non-abelian groups satisfying conditions (i)–(v) above and which provides the most appropriate platform for non-commutative group-based cryptographic schemes. More precisely: produce a class C of groups together with explicit normal-form and sampling algorithms, and a candidate hard problem P on groups in C, such that P is provably (or convincingly) resistant to all currently known generic attacks (length-based attacks, linear/representation attacks, quotient attacks, subgroup attacks), while still supporting the construction of secure key-exchange and public-key encryption protocols.

Design new digital signature schemes whose security relies on computational problems in non-commutative (non-abelian) groups or other non-commutative algebraic structures (such as group rings, semigroups, or non-commutative rings/algebras).

Concretely: a digital signature scheme is a triple of probabilistic polynomial-time algorithms (KeyGen, Sign, Verify) such that
- KeyGen outputs a public/private key pair (pk, sk);
- Sign(sk, m) outputs a signature σ on a message m;
- Verify(pk, m, σ) outputs accept or reject;
satisfying correctness (honestly generated signatures verify) and existential unforgeability under chosen-message attacks (EUF-CMA): no probabilistic polynomial-time adversary, given pk and oracle access to Sign(sk, ·), can with non-negligible probability output a pair (m*, σ*) with m* not queried such that Verify(pk, m*, σ*) accepts.

Call such a scheme "non-commutative" if its security reduces to a presumed-hard computational problem (e.g., conjugacy search, word problem, decomposition problem, subgroup membership, hidden subgroup problem, polynomial symmetrical decomposition, etc.) in an explicitly non-abelian platform structure G (a non-abelian group, semigroup, or non-commutative ring/algebra).

Task: Construct non-commutative digital signature schemes beyond the small number of previously known constructions, and rigorously analyze their correctness, efficiency, and EUF-CMA security based on a clearly stated hardness assumption in the chosen non-abelian platform.

The time-integrated spectra of the prompt gamma-ray emission from gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are commonly well fit by the empirical Band function, defined as a smoothly broken power law

  N(E) = A (E/100 keV)^α exp(-E/E0),  for E ≤ (α − β) E0,
  N(E) = A [(α − β) E0/100 keV]^(α−β) exp(β − α) (E/100 keV)^β,  for E > (α − β) E0,

with low-energy photon index α (typically α ≈ −1), high-energy photon index β (typically β ≈ −2 to −2.5), and a peak energy E_peak = (2 + α) E0 in the νF_ν representation typically of order 10²–10³ keV.

Determine which physical radiation mechanism is responsible for producing this prompt spectrum. The mechanism must self-consistently account for:
 (i) the observed distribution of low-energy spectral slopes α, including events that violate the synchrotron "line of death" (α > −2/3) and the fast-cooling synchrotron limit (α > −3/2);
 (ii) the inferred electron cooling regime, given that radiative cooling timescales are generally much shorter than the dynamical timescale;
 (iii) the observed values and clustering of the peak energy E_peak.

The candidate mechanisms to be discriminated among include:
 (a) optically thin synchrotron emission from accelerated leptons in internal shocks or magnetic reconnection sites;
 (b) jitter (small-scale random magnetic field) radiation from accelerated leptons;
 (c) synchrotron self-Compton emission;
 (d) Comptonization of thermal or photospheric (quasi-blackbody) seed photons by a dissipative photosphere;
 (e) hadronic radiation processes (e.g., proton synchrotron, photo-pion induced cascades).

Identify the dominant prompt-emission radiation mechanism, or establish a definitive multi-component framework, that simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iii) for the observed GRB population.

Let $f, g \in \mathbb{C}\{z\}$ be two holomorphic germs at the origin of the form
$$f(z) = \lambda z + a_{p+1} z^{p+1} + a_{p+2} z^{p+2} + \cdots, \qquad g(z) = \mu z + b_{q+1} z^{q+1} + b_{q+2} z^{q+2} + \cdots,$$
with $\lambda^n = 1$ and $\mu^m = 1$ for some positive integers $n, m$ (i.e., $f$ and $g$ are parabolic germs of one complex variable, with $0$ a fixed point whose multiplier is a root of unity). To each such parabolic germ one associates the following holomorphic conjugacy invariants:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the multiplier $f'(0)$;
  \item the parabolic multiplicity (the integer $\nu$ such that, after iterating to make the multiplier equal to $1$, the first nonvanishing higher-order term has degree $\nu+1$);
  \item the iterative residue (the formal residue at $0$ of $dz/(z - f^{\circ k}(z))$ for an appropriate iterate);
  \item the Écalle–Voronin functional invariants, i.e., the equivalence class of the tuple of horn maps obtained from the Fatou coordinates on the attracting and repelling petals, which together with the discrete data above form a complete system of invariants for local holomorphic conjugacy.
\end{itemize}
Two such germs $f$ and $g$ are said to be holomorphically locally conjugate if there exists a germ of biholomorphism $h \in \mathbb{C}\{z\}$ with $h(0) = 0$ and $h \circ f = g \circ h$ near $0$.

Question. Does there exist an effective decision procedure (an algorithm taking as input finite descriptions of $f$ and $g$, e.g. their convergent power-series coefficients given by computable approximations together with effective bounds) that, in finite time, decides whether $f$ and $g$ are holomorphically locally conjugate? In particular, can the comparison of the Écalle–Voronin functional invariants — which are equivalence classes of pairs of holomorphic horn maps and are generally regarded as intractable — be made algorithmically decidable?

In the Standard Model of particle physics together with its expected high-energy completions, identify the following characteristic energy scales:
- the QCD confinement scale Λ_QCD ≈ 200 MeV;
- the electroweak symmetry-breaking scale set by the Higgs vacuum expectation value v ≈ 246 GeV;
- the neutrino-mass-related (seesaw) scale M_ν;
- the Peccei–Quinn scale M_PQ associated with a solution to the strong CP problem;
- the grand-unification scale M_GUT ≈ 10^{16} GeV at which the SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1) gauge couplings are conjectured to unify;
- the Planck scale M_Pl ≈ 1.22 × 10^{19} GeV at which gravitational interactions become strong.

The Higgs sector contains a scalar field whose squared mass m_H^2 receives quadratically divergent radiative corrections of order Λ^2 from any ultraviolet cutoff Λ, and stabilizing m_H^2 ~ v^2 against contributions of order M_Pl^2 or M_GUT^2 through a bare counterterm requires a relative cancellation of order (v/M_Pl)^2 ≈ 10^{-34}.

Provide a fundamental, dynamical explanation of the gauge hierarchy by answering all three of the following:
(a) Origin: Why are the strong and electroweak scales (Λ_QCD, v) smaller than (M_ν, M_PQ, M_GUT, M_Pl) by many orders of magnitude?
(b) Stability: Why is the smallness of v/M_Pl natural under quantum corrections, i.e. why does the electroweak scale not require extreme fine tuning of bare parameters in the presence of much higher physical scales?
(c) Desert: Why does the spectrum appear to contain no new particles and no new interaction scales in the vast logarithmic interval between (Λ_QCD, v) and (M_ν, M_GUT, M_Pl)?

A satisfactory solution should consist of a physical mechanism (or proof of impossibility) consistent with all current experimental constraints that simultaneously accounts for (a), (b), and (c).

Let d ≥ 2 be an integer and let E = {±e_1, …, ±e_d} denote the set of unit vectors of Z^d. A cookie environment is a family ω = (ω(z, ·, ·))_{z ∈ Z^d}, where for each site z ∈ Z^d and each i ∈ N, ω(z, ·, i) is a probability vector on E (the "i-th cookie" at site z). Such an environment is sampled according to a probability measure P satisfying:

(IID) the family (ω(z, ·, ·))_{z ∈ Z^d} is i.i.d. under P;

(UEL) (uniform ellipticity) there exists κ > 0 such that, for all z ∈ Z^d, e ∈ E, and i ∈ N, P-a.s. ω(z, e, i) ≥ κ;

(BD) (bounded number of non-placebo cookies) there exists a deterministic M ∈ N such that, for all z ∈ Z^d, P-a.s. ω(z, ·, i) equals the uniform distribution on E for every i > M.

Given a realization of ω, the excited random walk (ERW) starting at 0 is the process (X_n)_{n ≥ 0} on Z^d with X_0 = 0 such that, conditionally on the past, if the walker is at z ∈ Z^d and this is its i-th visit to z, it jumps to z + e with probability ω(z, e, i). Let P_0 denote the resulting annealed law of (X_n) starting from 0.

For a nonzero direction ℓ ∈ R^d \ {0}, define the event
A_ℓ := { lim_{n → ∞} X_n · ℓ = ∞ }.

Question: Under the assumptions (IID), (UEL), and (BD), does the zero-one law
P_0[A_ℓ] ∈ {0, 1}
hold for every ℓ ∈ R^d \ {0}? More generally, identify conditions on the cookie environment that imply this dichotomy.

Consider an interacting system of $N$ identical fermions evolving under a (possibly time-dependent) Hamiltonian. For each $p\ge 1$, let $\rho_p(t)$ denote the time-dependent $p$-body reduced density matrix obtained by tracing the full $N$-body density matrix over $N-p$ particles. The matrices $\rho_p(t)$ satisfy the BBGKY hierarchy, in which the equation of motion for $\rho_p(t)$ couples to $\rho_{p+1}(t)$.

A closure (truncation) at the two-body level is a prescription that expresses $\rho_3(t)$ as a functional $\mathcal{F}[\rho_1(t),\rho_2(t)]$ so that the coupled equations for $\rho_1(t)$ and $\rho_2(t)$ become a closed system. Call such a closure admissible if, for every initial datum $(\rho_1(0),\rho_2(0))$ arising from a fermionic $N$-body pure or mixed state and for all $t\ge 0$, the propagated matrices $\rho_1(t)$ and $\rho_2(t)$ satisfy:

(i) Positive semidefiniteness: $\rho_1(t)\succeq 0$ and $\rho_2(t)\succeq 0$;

(ii) Fermionic occupation constraint: every eigenvalue (natural orbital occupation number) of $\rho_1(t)$ lies in $[0,1]$;

(iii) Physical local density: the diagonal $n(x,t)=\rho_1(x,x;t)$ is non-negative everywhere and remains finite (no divergences) for all $t\ge 0$;

(iv) Conservation of trace (particle number) and Hermiticity of $\rho_1(t),\rho_2(t)$.

Problem. Construct a non-trivial closure functional $\mathcal{F}[\rho_1,\rho_2]$ for the truncated two-body BBGKY hierarchy that is admissible in the above sense (in particular, that goes beyond mean-field/Hartree–Fock and includes genuine two-body correlations), and prove that the resulting closed evolution propagates the constraints (i)–(iv) exactly, without recourse to ad hoc post-hoc purification or filtering.

Background. For an infinite cardinal κ, write c = 2^{ℵ_0} for the cardinality of the continuum. For cardinals α,β,γ,δ, the strong polarized partition relation
   ⎛ α ⎞      ⎛ γ ⎞^{1,1}
   ⎝ β ⎠  →  ⎝ δ ⎠_{2}
asserts that for every coloring c : α × β → 2 there exist A ⊆ α with |A| = γ and B ⊆ β with |B| = δ such that c is constant on A × B.

For an uncountable cardinal κ, say that κ belongs to the positive spectrum (of strong polarized relations) if
   ⎛ κ ⎞      ⎛ κ ⎞^{1,1}
   ⎝ ω ⎠  →  ⎝ ω ⎠_{2}
holds. A cardinal κ is called a breaking point of the positive spectrum if κ lies in the positive spectrum but there exists a cardinal μ < κ that does not lie in the positive spectrum (equivalently, κ marks an "upward jump" in the spectrum from a failure to a success).

Question. 
(a) Is it consistent with ZFC that ℵ_ω is the unique breaking point of the positive spectrum?
(b) More generally, is it consistent with ZFC that the continuum c = 2^{ℵ_0} is arbitrarily large while the set of breaking points of the positive spectrum coincides exactly with the class of cardinals of countable cofinality?

A permutation class is a set C of (finite) permutations that is closed under taking pattern-subpermutations: if σ ∈ C and τ is contained in σ as a pattern, then τ ∈ C. Every permutation class C is determined by its (possibly infinite) basis B(C), the antichain of minimal permutations not in C; the class C is called finitely based if B(C) is finite. For a permutation class C, let c_n denote the number of permutations of length n in C, and define the (upper) growth rate gr(C) = limsup_{n→∞} c_n^{1/n}.

Let Γ denote the set of growth rates of all permutation classes, and let Γ_fb ⊆ Γ denote the set of growth rates of finitely based permutation classes. Balogh, Bollobás and Morris formulated conjectures concerning the structure of Γ; among their predictions are the following:
(i) every real number that is a growth rate of a permutation class is algebraic;
(ii) for every real α, the set Γ ∩ [0, α] is well-ordered (in particular, contains no accumulation points from above and no perfect subsets);
(iii) Γ is countable.

These conjectures are known to be false for general permutation classes. Determine whether the analogues of these conjectures hold when Γ is replaced everywhere by Γ_fb; that is, decide whether every growth rate of a finitely based permutation class is algebraic, whether Γ_fb is well-ordered below every bound, and whether Γ_fb is countable.

Consider an infinite-dimensional Hamiltonian system whose linearization at an equilibrium is given by a self-adjoint operator $L$ (for instance, $L = -\Delta_f + v$, a Schrödinger-type operator on a non-compact domain such as $\mathbb{R}^d$, where $\Delta_f$ is a Laplacian and $v$ is a potential). Assume:

1. The spectrum of $L$ is pure point, consisting of a sequence of nonzero real eigenvalues (the normal frequencies) $\{\lambda_n\}_{n\ge 1}$;
2. A subset of these eigenvalues is dense in some bounded interval $[a,b] \subset \mathbb{R}$ (for example, the eigenvalues might include all rational numbers in $[1,2]$);
3. The full Hamiltonian has the form $H = H_0 + P$, where $H_0$ is the quadratic part associated with $L$ and $P$ is a nonlinear perturbation that is not of short range — meaning, in the PDE setting on the whole space, that $P$ does not decay rapidly in the spatial variable and is not localized.

Question: Under these assumptions, do there exist KAM (Kolmogorov–Arnold–Moser) invariant tori for the Hamiltonian flow generated by $H$? More precisely, does there exist a positive-measure family of finite-dimensional invariant tori on which the flow is conjugate to a linear quasi-periodic flow with Diophantine frequency vectors selected from the normal frequencies $\{\lambda_n\}$?

In ultra-relativistic heavy-ion collisions, a "centrality class" is an equivalence class of events grouped by collision geometry. Theoretical models typically classify events by the impact parameter b (the transverse distance between the centers of the two colliding nuclei) or by initial-state observables such as the number of participating nucleons N_part or the number of produced gluons N_g at proper time τ_0. Experimental analyses, in contrast, classify events by final-state observables, most commonly the charged-particle multiplicity dN_ch/dη or the transverse energy dE_T/dη at midrapidity, after the full dynamical evolution of the system.

Let P(b) denote the geometric probability density of impact parameters. Let N_g(b) be a (model-dependent) initial gluon number, let s_0(b,x_⊥) denote an initial entropy density profile produced at τ_0, and let the subsequent evolution be described by relativistic viscous hydrodynamics with shear viscosity over entropy density η/s and bulk viscosity ζ/s, followed by hadronic freeze-out yielding a final charged multiplicity N_ch. Because the evolution is dissipative, the total entropy S(τ) = ∫ s(τ,x) d^3x is not conserved: S(τ_f) > S(τ_0).

Formulate a procedure for defining and matching centrality classes between theory and experiment that satisfies all of the following requirements:

(a) The mapping b ↦ N_g(b) ↦ S(τ_0;b) ↦ S(τ_f;b) ↦ N_ch(b) is specified explicitly, including its event-by-event fluctuations, and the cumulative distribution of the chosen classifier on the theory side is matched to the cumulative distribution of the experimental classifier;

(b) The amount of dissipative entropy production during hydrodynamic evolution is quantified and incorporated, so that classifying by initial entropy or by final multiplicity yields consistent (and quantitatively related) centrality bins;

(c) Any assumed proportionality between b, N_part, N_g, S(τ_0), and N_ch is verified rather than assumed, with errors controlled;

(d) The resulting comparison between computed and measured observables (e.g., anisotropic flow coefficients v_n) is sufficiently sensitive to extract the QGP transport coefficients η/s and ζ/s with quantifiable systematic uncertainty arising from the centrality definition itself.

Provide such a matching prescription, prove that it minimizes the systematic bias on the extracted transport coefficients across plausible choices of initial-condition model, and characterize the residual uncertainty as a function of centrality.

In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the azimuthal distribution of produced particles in a given event can be expanded in a Fourier series, and the harmonic flow coefficients v_n are extracted from two-particle correlations. Define the two-particle Fourier coefficient V_{nΔ}(p_T^a, p_T^t) for trigger transverse momentum p_T^t and associated transverse momentum p_T^a as the n-th Fourier coefficient of the pair azimuthal distribution; one says that V_{nΔ} factorizes if V_{nΔ}(p_T^a, p_T^t) = v_n(p_T^a) v_n(p_T^t). Let Ψ_m denote the m-th-order event plane, and define v_n{Ψ_m} = ⟨cos(n(φ − Ψ_m))⟩, the n-th harmonic flow coefficient measured with respect to Ψ_m.

Devise and analyze a concrete program of measurements and observables that:

(a) tests factorization of V_{nΔ} as a function of (p_T^a, p_T^t) over a wide kinematic range, in order to separate collective bulk anisotropies from non-flow contributions and to characterize event-by-event flow fluctuations;

(b) extends factorization tests to the space of trigger and associated pseudorapidity (η^a, η^t), formulating the appropriate two-particle observable V_{nΔ}(η^a, η^t) and identifying the signatures that would distinguish flow from non-flow in pseudorapidity;

(c) uses v_n{Ψ_m} with m ≠ n (mixed-harmonic event-plane correlations) to probe the hydrodynamic response and quantify mode mixing between initial-state anisotropies ε_m and final-state harmonics v_n.

Specify the observables, their expected behavior under pure flow versus non-flow scenarios, and a procedure for using them to disentangle bulk anisotropies, flow fluctuations, and hydrodynamic mode mixing.

Let G be a finite metric graph, i.e. a connected graph whose edges are identified with intervals of R (some possibly half-lines), endowed with the natural arc-length measure. On G consider the focusing nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation
   i ∂_t Ψ = -Δ_V Ψ - |Ψ|^{2σ} Ψ,    σ > 0,
where Δ_V denotes the Laplacian on G defined by a self-adjoint vertex coupling V at each vertex v. The standard Kirchhoff (free) coupling requires continuity of the wavefunction at v together with the vanishing of the sum of the outgoing derivatives. More general self-adjoint couplings include:
 (a) δ-couplings of strength α: continuity of Ψ at v and Σ ∂Ψ_e(v) = α Ψ(v);
 (b) δ′-couplings: continuity of the outgoing derivatives at v and a jump condition Σ Ψ_e(v) = β ∂Ψ(v);
 (c) more general U(d)-parametrized couplings at a vertex of degree d.
A standing wave is a solution of the form Ψ(t,x) = e^{iωt} φ(x) with φ ∈ H^1(G) a real-valued bound state of the stationary equation -Δ_V φ + ω φ = |φ|^{2σ} φ; a ground state is a minimizer of the associated NLS energy under fixed L^2-mass.

Question: For NLS on a metric graph G equipped with non-Kirchhoff vertex conditions (in particular δ- or δ′-type couplings, or other self-adjoint couplings), determine whether such couplings give rise to:
 (i) new branches of standing waves not present in the Kirchhoff case;
 (ii) bifurcations of the ground state as the mass (or frequency) parameter varies;
 (iii) spontaneous symmetry-breaking phenomena, in analogy with the known symmetry-breaking bifurcation of the ground state established for the NLS on the real line with a δ′ point interaction.

Characterize, in terms of the graph topology and the vertex coupling parameters, the existence, multiplicity, and orbital (in)stability of such standing-wave branches and bifurcation points.

Hutchinson's paradox of the plankton concerns the apparent contradiction between two facts: (i) the principle of competitive exclusion asserts that, at equilibrium, the number of coexisting species cannot exceed the number of limiting resources, so when many phytoplankton species compete for only one or a few limiting nutrients, only one or a few species should survive; (ii) in real aquatic ecosystems hundreds of phytoplankton species are observed to coexist while competing for only one or a few limiting nutrients.

Construct and analyze a nonequilibrium reaction–advection–diffusion model of the following form. Let f(t,x) ≥ 0 denote the density of a single limiting nutrient and let ρ_n(t,x) ≥ 0, n = 1, 2, …, N, denote the densities of N distinct phytoplankton species, where x lies in a bounded fluid domain Ω ⊂ ℝ^d (d = 2 or 3) with suitable boundary conditions. Suppose:

(a) the water carries a prescribed (possibly turbulent) velocity field u(t,x) advecting both the nutrient and the species;
(b) each species n drifts relative to the water with a species-specific velocity v_n(t,x), modeling buoyancy or motility;
(c) the nutrient is regenerated by a source term S(t,x,f) and consumed by uptake of the form Σ_n U_n(f) ρ_n, where U_n is a species-dependent uptake function (e.g. of Monod type);
(d) species n grows at a rate G_n(f) ρ_n − m_n ρ_n with species-dependent growth function G_n and mortality m_n;
(e) molecular or eddy diffusion with coefficients D_f, D_n ≥ 0 is included.

The coupled system is, schematically,
  ∂_t f + ∇·(u f) = D_f Δf + S(t,x,f) − Σ_n U_n(f) ρ_n,
  ∂_t ρ_n + ∇·((u + v_n) ρ_n) = D_n Δρ_n + (G_n(f) − m_n) ρ_n,  n = 1,…,N.

Question: Can such a nonequilibrium model exhibit, for natural choices of u, v_n, S, U_n, G_n, sustained long-time coexistence of an arbitrarily large number N of species (in the sense that, for example, lim inf_{t→∞} ∫_Ω ρ_n(t,x) dx > 0 for every n), thereby providing a mechanistic resolution of the paradox of the plankton? More precisely, identify conditions on the velocity fields, nutrient regeneration, uptake and growth functions under which competitive exclusion fails and persistent coexistence of many species occurs.

Let $M$ be a closed (compact, without boundary) smooth manifold of dimension at least $2$, and for an integer $k \geq 2$ let $\mathcal{G}^{k}(M)$ denote the space of smooth Riemannian metrics on $M$ equipped with the $C^{k}$-topology. For a metric $g \in \mathcal{G}^{k}(M)$, let $\varphi^{g}_{t}$ denote its geodesic flow on the unit tangent bundle $SM$, and let $h_{\mathrm{top}}(\varphi^{g})$ denote its topological entropy.

Determine whether each of the following sets is dense in $\mathcal{G}^{k}(M)$:

(i) the set of metrics $g$ for which $h_{\mathrm{top}}(\varphi^{g}) > 0$;

(ii) (assuming $\dim M \geq 3$) the set of metrics $g$ for which the geodesic flow $\varphi^{g}$ is transitive, i.e. there exists an orbit whose closure is all of $SM$;

(iii) the set of metrics $g$ for which the union of tangent vectors to closed geodesics is dense in $SM$.

Let $(M,g)$ be a closed (compact, without boundary) connected smooth pseudo-Riemannian manifold. Suppose there exists a $(1,1)$-tensor field $L$ on $M$ (equivalently, a smooth field of endomorphisms $L:TM\to TM$) such that:
(i) $L\not\equiv 0$;
(ii) $L$ is $g$-self-adjoint, i.e. $g(LX,Y)=g(X,LY)$ for all vector fields $X,Y$;
(iii) $L$ is parallel with respect to the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ of $g$, i.e. $\nabla L=0$;
(iv) $L$ is nilpotent, i.e. $L^k=0$ at every point of $M$ for some integer $k\ge 2$.

Question: Do such pairs $(M,g,L)$ exist? More precisely, classify (or determine constraints on) closed pseudo-Riemannian manifolds admitting a non-zero parallel, $g$-self-adjoint, nilpotent $(1,1)$-tensor field. (Note that in the Riemannian signature, any $g$-self-adjoint nilpotent endomorphism must vanish identically, so the question is meaningful only in indefinite signature.)

Active galactic nuclei (AGN) are accreting supermassive black holes at the centers of galaxies, some of which launch relativistic jets that can produce high-energy gamma-ray emission. Two classes of jetted AGN have long been established as gamma-ray emitters: (i) blazars (BL Lac objects and flat-spectrum radio quasars), characterized by relativistic jets pointed close to the line of sight, hosted in giant elliptical galaxies and powered by very massive black holes (typically 10^8–10^9 solar masses); and (ii) radio galaxies, the misaligned-jet counterparts of blazars, also typically hosted in giant ellipticals.

A third class of gamma-ray–emitting jetted AGN consists of narrow-line Seyfert 1 (NLS1) galaxies, defined by the optical spectral criteria: full width at half maximum of the broad Hβ line FWHM(Hβ) < 2000 km/s, a flux ratio [O III]/Hβ < 3, and strong Fe II emission. NLS1 galaxies are generally believed to host comparatively low-mass black holes (10^6–10^8 solar masses) accreting at high Eddington ratios. A radio-loud subset of NLS1s has been detected in gamma rays.

Problem. Provide a precise comparative characterization of gamma-ray–emitting NLS1 galaxies versus blazars and versus radio galaxies along the following axes:
(a) host-galaxy morphology and large-scale environment;
(b) central black hole mass distribution and Eddington accretion ratio;
(c) jet power, bulk Lorentz factor, viewing angle, and overall jet structure;
(d) broadband spectral energy distribution shape (in particular the location of synchrotron and inverse-Compton peaks) and the relative contributions of disc/corona versus jet emission;
(e) variability properties across the electromagnetic spectrum;
(f) cosmological evolution and possible evolutionary status (e.g., whether gamma-ray NLS1s constitute a genuinely distinct population or a low-mass, high-accretion, "young" analogue of flat-spectrum radio quasars).

In each case, identify the quantitative differences and similarities and assess whether the differences are intrinsic or arise from selection and orientation effects.

For any class $\mathcal{X}$ of groups, define the following operators:
- $H(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all homomorphic images of groups in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $S(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to a subgroup of some group in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $P(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to an (unrestricted) direct product $\prod_{i\in I} G_i$ of an arbitrary family $(G_i)_{i\in I}$ of groups in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $P_f(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to a direct product of a finite family of groups in $\mathcal{X}$.

By Birkhoff's HSP theorem, $H(S(P(\mathcal{X})))$ is the variety of groups generated by $\mathcal{X}$. A group $G$ is called metabelian if its commutator subgroup $[G,G]$ is abelian (equivalently, if $G$ has an abelian normal subgroup with abelian quotient).

Question: Let $\mathcal{M}$ be any class of metabelian groups. Must the inclusion
$$H(S(P_f(\mathcal{M}))) \;\subseteq\; S(H(P(S(\mathcal{M}))))$$
hold?

Work in the model-theoretic category of groups of finite Morley rank. Recall that a group of finite Morley rank is a group equipped with a structure in which every definable set has a well-defined finite ordinal-valued Morley rank, and a "definable" subset/subgroup means one defined by a first-order formula with parameters. A group is called connected if it has no proper definable subgroup of finite index.

Let S and M be groups of finite Morley rank. Assume:
(i) S is infinite and simple;
(ii) every proper definable subgroup of S is nilpotent;
(iii) M is non-trivial and connected, and acts on S definably and faithfully (so M embeds definably into the group of definable automorphisms of S);
(iv) the action of M on S is irreducible, meaning that M leaves invariant no definable subgroup of S other than {1} and S itself.

Prove that under these hypotheses the action of M on S is equivalent to the action of S on itself by inner automorphisms (conjugation); equivalently, there is a definable isomorphism between M and S (or its image in Inn(S) = S) that intertwines the given action of M on S with the conjugation action of S on S.

A Golod group is a finitely generated infinite residually finite p-group (for some prime p) every finitely generated subgroup of which is finite, constructed via the Golod–Shafarevich inequality applied to a graded associative algebra with few relations; equivalently, an infinite finitely generated p-group obtained as a subgroup of the adjoint group 1+J of a nil algebra J of Golod–Shafarevich type, so that every finitely generated subgroup is a finite p-group while the whole group is infinite.

An AT-group (in the sense of Rozhkov) is an infinite group G acting faithfully by automorphisms on a spherically homogeneous rooted tree T such that:
(i) G acts level-transitively on T;
(ii) every element of G has finite order (G is a periodic group);
(iii) the stabilizers in G of vertices of T satisfy a prescribed self-similar/branch condition making G a so-called "AT" (Aleshin-type / "almost-tree") group, namely G is generated by finitely many automorphisms of T whose action on each subtree below the first level is again, up to a permutation of subtrees, an element of (a fixed system of) generators of G.

Problem. Exhibit a Golod group Γ, a subgroup H ≤ Γ, an infinite AT-group A in the sense above, and a surjective group homomorphism φ : H → A. Equivalently: prove that some Golod group has a subgroup that maps homomorphically onto an infinite AT-group, and construct such a triple (Γ, H, φ) explicitly.

Consider the time-reversal-invariant Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE), defined as the ensemble of real symmetric N×N random matrices H=(H_{ij}) whose entries are independent Gaussian random variables (up to symmetry H_{ij}=H_{ji}) with mean zero, variance v^2(1+δ_{ij}), and joint distribution invariant under orthogonal transformations. In the limit N→∞, the GOE predicts a number of universal statistical signatures for the spectrum of a complex quantum system, including:

(a) the Wigner surmise for the distribution of nearest-neighbor level spacings (after unfolding to unit mean spacing), P(s)=(π/2)s exp(−π s^2/4);

(b) the Dyson–Mehta Δ_3(L) statistic, measuring spectral rigidity over a stretch of L consecutive unfolded levels, with the asymptotic GOE form Δ_3(L) ≈ (1/π^2)[ln(2π L)+γ−5/4−π^2/8] for large L;

(c) the Porter–Thomas distribution for partial decay widths Γ, namely a chi-squared distribution with one degree of freedom: P(Γ) dΓ = (2π Γ ⟨Γ⟩)^{−1/2} exp(−Γ/(2⟨Γ⟩)) dΓ, obtained from the assumption that the components of an eigenvector along any fixed channel vector are independent Gaussian random variables.

Now consider the experimental compound-nucleus resonance data: large sets of neutron and proton resonances of fixed spin and parity (so-called quantum-number-clean sequences) measured for medium and heavy nuclei, together with their resonance positions (giving spacings) and reduced partial widths (in particular the reduced neutron widths Γ_n^0).

Question: Are the GOE predictions (a), (b), and (c) consistent with the experimental data on compound-nucleus resonances—across nearest-neighbor spacing distributions, the long-range Δ_3(L) rigidity for long resonance sequences, and the distribution of reduced neutron widths—at a level of agreement strong enough to justify random-matrix theory as a physically meaningful description of the compound-nucleus regime in nuclear reaction theory?

Let $A$ be a finite alphabet. Define the class of extended regular expressions over $A$ inductively: the symbols $0$ and $1$ are extended regular expressions, every letter $a \in A$ is an extended regular expression, and if $E$ and $F$ are extended regular expressions then so are $E \cup F$ (union), $E \cdot F$ (concatenation), $E^c$ (complement, with respect to $A^*$), and $E^*$ (Kleene star). Each such expression denotes a language over $A$ in the standard way; the languages denoted by extended regular expressions are exactly the regular languages over $A$.

Define the (generalized) star height of an extended regular expression $E$ recursively by:
- $h(0) = h(1) = h(a) = 0$ for every $a \in A$;
- $h(E \cup F) = h(E \cdot F) = \max(h(E), h(F))$;
- $h(E^c) = h(E)$;
- $h(E^*) = h(E) + 1$.

For a regular language $L \subseteq A^*$, define its (generalized) star height $h(L)$ to be the minimum of $h(E)$ taken over all extended regular expressions $E$ denoting $L$.

Question: Does there exist an algorithm which, given as input an arbitrary regular language $L$ (specified, e.g., by a finite automaton or an extended regular expression), computes the integer $h(L)$?

Consider the theoretical description of compound-nuclear (CN) reactions, in which an incident projectile and a target nucleus form a long-lived intermediate compound system whose decay proceeds via numerous overlapping or isolated resonances. Two well-developed limiting frameworks exist:

(i) A purely statistical treatment (e.g., Hauser–Feshbach theory with width-fluctuation corrections), valid when the average level spacing D of CN resonances at the relevant excitation energy is much smaller than the average resonance width Γ, so that the resonances strongly overlap and an ensemble/average description in terms of transmission coefficients and level densities is appropriate.

(ii) An individual resonance-by-resonance treatment (e.g., R-matrix analysis), valid when the number of relevant resonances is small enough that each resonance's energy, partial widths, and spin–parity assignments can be determined and used explicitly.

Develop a quantitative reaction theory that consistently bridges the intermediate regime where neither limit is appropriate: namely, situations in which the density of CN resonances is too low to justify averaging assumptions of the statistical model (so that strong fluctuations in cross sections survive and Porter–Thomas/Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble averages are unreliable), yet the number of contributing resonances is too large to be treated explicitly one by one. Such regimes arise for reactions on light nuclei and on nuclei far from the valley of beta-stability (where particle separation energies, level densities, and isospin structure differ markedly from stable medium-mass nuclei).

Specifically, formulate a framework that:
(a) provides cross sections and their fluctuations/uncertainties as functions of incident energy in this intermediate-density regime;
(b) reduces, in appropriate limits, to the statistical (Hauser–Feshbach) result when D ≪ Γ and to an R-matrix-type description when only a few resonances contribute;
(c) accounts for direct-reaction couplings, isospin mixing, and pre-equilibrium contributions relevant to light and exotic nuclei;
(d) yields predictions usable for systems where complete spectroscopic information on individual CN resonances is not available.

Consider neutrino-induced exclusive strangeness-production processes on nuclear targets at intermediate energies (incident neutrino energies of order 1–10 GeV), in which a strange hadron (e.g. a kaon or hyperon) is produced together with possibly other final-state particles. The measured cross section on a nucleus differs from the elementary neutrino–nucleon strangeness-production cross section because of three classes of nuclear effects:

(i) nuclear dynamics of the initial state, including the bound-nucleon momentum distribution, binding energy, Pauli blocking, and short-range correlations;
(ii) final-state interactions (FSI) of the produced strange and non-strange hadrons as they propagate through the residual nucleus, including elastic and inelastic rescattering, charge exchange, and absorption;
(iii) secondary production mechanisms in which strange hadrons are produced not in the primary neutrino–nucleon vertex but through subsequent hadronic interactions inside the nucleus (e.g. associated production by reinteracting pions or nucleons, hyperon–nucleon strangeness exchange).

Develop a quantitative model (analytic, Monte Carlo, or hybrid) of these three effects that is sufficiently reliable, with controlled and validated systematic uncertainties, that exclusive strangeness-production cross sections measured on nuclear targets can be unfolded to recover the underlying elementary neutrino–nucleon strangeness-production cross sections (such as ν_μ n → μ⁻ Λ K⁺, ν̄_μ p → μ⁺ Λ, ν̄_μ p → μ⁺ K⁻ p, etc.) with quantified accuracy. Specify the required theoretical inputs (spectral functions, hyperon– and kaon–nucleon interaction cross sections, in-medium modifications) and a procedure for extracting the elementary cross sections together with rigorous uncertainty estimates from nuclear-target data.

Consider Monte Carlo event generators used to simulate charged- and neutral-current neutrino-nucleus scattering at intermediate energies (roughly 0.1-10 GeV), where the inclusive cross section receives contributions from quasielastic single-nucleon knockout, two-particle two-hole (multinucleon) excitations driven by meson-exchange currents and short-range correlations, resonant and non-resonant single- and multi-pion production, deep inelastic scattering, and is further modified by nuclear ground-state dynamics (e.g. spectral functions, random phase approximation, relativistic mean field) and by hadronic final-state interactions in the residual nucleus.

Devise a consistent and computationally efficient framework for incorporating modern microscopic theoretical descriptions of all of the above channels into a unified Monte Carlo event generator that:

(a) treats each exclusive hadronic final state with kinematics and interference effects compatible with the underlying inclusive cross-section model (i.e., no double counting between quasielastic, two-particle two-hole, and pion-production channels, and a coherent treatment of axial-vector form factors of the nucleon and of baryon resonances);

(b) couples the primary interaction vertex to a quantum-mechanically consistent intranuclear cascade or transport model for final-state interactions, with a clearly specified prescription for off-shell propagation, Pauli blocking, and medium modifications;

(c) provides well-defined, theoretically motivated tunable parameters (rather than ad hoc rescalings of axial masses or normalization factors) that experimental collaborations can constrain from data without sacrificing predictivity;

(d) is efficient enough to generate the large event samples (>= 10^8 events) required for precision neutrino-oscillation analyses.

Formulate such a framework explicitly, prove (or argue rigorously) that it reduces to the established microscopic inclusive cross sections in the appropriate limits, and demonstrate that fits to existing neutrino-nucleus scattering data do not require unphysical parameter values.

Solar prominences are cool, dense plasma structures suspended in the hot solar corona by magnetic fields. High-resolution observations reveal dark "bubbles" rising from below the prominence body and developing into upward "plumes" that propagate through the prominence material. Three physical scenarios have been proposed to account for these features:

(i) the bubbles are filled with hot coronal plasma that accumulates in a cavity beneath the prominence and subsequently undergoes condensation, the bubble–prominence interface being subject to a buoyancy-driven (e.g. magnetic Rayleigh–Taylor) instability;

(ii) the rising structures are produced by a thermal instability operating within the prominence–corona system;

(iii) the bubbles correspond to magnetic separatrix surfaces surrounding regions of newly emerging magnetic flux beneath the prominence, in which case the apparent dark rising features are not filled with hot plasma but are simply line-of-sight "windows" through the prominence sheet that reveal the background corona.

Determine which of these three mechanisms — (i) hot plasma filling a cavity prior to condensation, (ii) thermal instability, or (iii) a separatrix around emerging magnetic flux producing optically thin windows — is responsible for the observed bubbles and rising plumes in quiescent solar prominences. In particular, decide whether the plasma inside an observed bubble is hot and dense enough to be physically distinct from the background corona, or whether the bubble interior is consistent with simply being unobscured background corona.

Consider a quiescent solar prominence whose fine structure, as seen in the 304 Å He II channel, contains oblique thread-like features aligned with magnetic field lines that lie along large coronal arches. These threads display rapid plasma motions of cool (chromospheric-temperature, ~10^4 K) material, with apparent opposite-direction (counter-streaming) flows along neighboring threads, embedded in a hotter EUV-emitting (~10^5–10^6 K) environment. Three candidate physical mechanisms have been proposed to account for these observed motions:

(i) rapid radiative cooling and condensation of EUV-emitting coronal plasma into cool material that then streams along the inclined field lines;
(ii) longitudinal oscillations of cool plasma trapped in magnetic dips, in which neighboring dipped flux tubes oscillate with different phases so that material travels toward one footpoint on some threads and toward the opposite footpoint on others, producing apparent counter-streaming;
(iii) large-scale pressure-driven siphon flows between the two footpoints of the supporting magnetic structure when these footpoints are rooted in regions of unequal magnetic field strength (and hence unequal chromospheric pressure), producing net mass transport from one end of the prominence to the other.

Determine which of mechanisms (i), (ii), (iii) — singly or in combination — is responsible for the fast cool-plasma flows and the observed counter-streaming along the oblique threads of large arch-like prominences. In particular, identify quantitative observational and theoretical criteria (characteristic velocities, timescales, thermal evolution, footpoint magnetic asymmetry, oscillation periods) that discriminate among the three scenarios, and decide whether the dominant mechanism for large-scale prominence structures is siphon flow rather than oscillation-induced counter-streaming or condensation from the EUV phase.

Consider a cosmological framework in which galaxies form within dark matter halos and, upon being accreted into a more massive host halo, become satellite galaxies. Each galaxy is associated with a hot gas reservoir that supplies cold gas for star formation through cooling. Observations indicate that:

(i) star-forming satellite galaxies form stars at rates comparable to central galaxies of the same stellar mass, implying that the transition from a star-forming to a passive state is rapid;
(ii) the bimodal distribution of specific star formation rates (sSFR) in galaxies, with relatively few galaxies at intermediate sSFR, must be reproduced by any successful model;
(iii) satellites must therefore sustain significant star formation for several Gyr after accretion, then quench on a comparatively short timescale.

Existing semi-analytic and hydrodynamical galaxy-formation models typically prescribe either instantaneous removal of the hot gas reservoir at infall or gradual ram-pressure / tidal stripping of that reservoir. Both prescriptions tend to produce satellite star-formation histories that decline too quickly after accretion and/or overpopulate the intermediate-sSFR (green-valley) region of the galaxy population.

Problem. Formulate and analyze a physically motivated model of gas processing in satellite galaxies—specifying the evolution of the hot gas reservoir, cold gas supply, star formation, and feedback after infall—such that the resulting population of satellites simultaneously:
(a) maintains star formation rates comparable to central galaxies of the same stellar mass for several Gyr after accretion;
(b) subsequently quenches on a timescale short enough that the predicted sSFR distribution reproduces the observed paucity of galaxies with intermediate specific star formation rates;
(c) reproduces the observed dependence of the satellite quenched fraction on stellar mass, halo mass, and time since infall.

Determine whether such a "delayed-then-rapid" quenching behavior can be achieved by relaxing the assumption of instantaneous hot-gas stripping alone, or whether additional physical processes (e.g. modified cooling, feedback regulation, stripping of cold gas) are necessary, and quantify the required parameters.

Let $f_\tau:(\mathbb{C}^n,\mathbb{R}^n)\to(\mathbb{C},\mathbb{R})$, $\tau\in(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$, be a one-parameter family of real analytic functions (or polynomials) realizing a Morse birth surgery, namely:

(i) for every $\tau\in(-\varepsilon,0)$, $f_\tau$ has, in a fixed neighborhood $U\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ of the origin, exactly two non-real critical points which are complex conjugate to each other;

(ii) as $\tau\to 0^-$ these two critical points collide at a single real degenerate critical point of type $A_2$;

(iii) for every $\tau\in(0,\varepsilon)$, $f_\tau$ has in $U$ exactly two real Morse critical points whose Morse indices are two consecutive integers $k$ and $k+1$.

For $\tau<0$, the two complex-conjugate vanishing cycles in the Milnor fiber of $f_\tau$ at the conjugate critical points carry a well-defined intersection number whose sign determines the parity of the larger Morse index $k+1$ of the newborn real critical points.

Problem. Find a topological invariant of the function $f_{-\varepsilon}$ (e.g.\ a characteristic constructed from its real or complex topology, vanishing cycles, monodromy, or discriminant data) which determines the integer Morse indices $k$ and $k+1$ of the two real critical points born at $\tau=0^+$, and not merely the parity of the larger one.

Let G = (V, E) be an undirected graph (a protein–protein interaction network), where V is a set of proteins and E ⊆ V × V is the set of experimentally observed pairwise interactions. Suppose V is partitioned into a subset M ⊆ V of membrane proteins and its complement V \ M of non-membrane proteins, where membership in M is determined by independent biological annotation (e.g., predicted transmembrane domains or subcellular localization). Define the membrane subinteractome as the induced subgraph G[M] = (M, E ∩ (M × M)). A membrane protein complex is a subset C ⊆ M whose elements form a stable physical assembly; the goal is to recover such complexes as densely connected subgraphs of G[M], given that only a very small reference set R of membrane complexes with fully characterized internal interactions is available for training or validation.

Develop a computational/graph-theoretic procedure that:

(i) extracts from G a reliable approximation Ĝ_M of the true membrane subinteractome, taking into account that direct membership annotation of M may be noisy and that interactions in E may have varying confidence weights w : E → [0, 1];

(ii) given Ĝ_M, partitions or covers M by candidate complexes {C_1, …, C_k} (possibly overlapping) that maximize internal connectivity/cohesion while remaining consistent with the small reference set R;

(iii) provides quantitative performance guarantees or evaluation criteria (precision, recall, or matching scores against R) in the regime where |R| ≪ k.

Formulate, analyze, and justify such a procedure, addressing in particular how the scarcity of characterized membrane complexes (|R| small) can be overcome — for example via transfer of structural patterns from the soluble subinteractome G[V \ M], via semi-supervised or unsupervised graph clustering on Ĝ_M, or via incorporation of auxiliary biological features as edge/vertex weights.

Consider a protoplanetary "transition disc," i.e., a circumstellar accretion disc around a young pre-main-sequence star whose continuum emission at millimeter wavelengths exhibits a resolved central cavity (a region of strongly reduced dust surface density extending from the star out to a cavity-edge radius R_cav of order tens of astronomical units), while the disc beyond R_cav remains millimeter-bright. Such "mm-bright" transition discs are observed to retain detectable gas (e.g., CO isotopologue line emission, near-infrared CO ro-vibrational lines, and accretion signatures onto the central star) inside the dust cavity, but spatially resolved sub-millimeter line observations also indicate that the gas surface density Σ_gas(r) inside R_cav is reduced relative to a smooth extrapolation of the outer-disc profile by factors typically in the range 10–10^4, with the magnitude and radial shape of the drop varying among sources and tracers.

Provide a single, internally consistent physical model of the gas component inside the dust cavities of mm-bright transition discs that simultaneously specifies:

(a) the radial profile of the gas surface density Σ_gas(r) for r < R_cav, including the location, depth, and width of any gas density drop(s);
(b) the gas temperature structure T_gas(r,z) and ionization/chemical state determining the abundances of the observed tracers (CO and its isotopologues, atomic and ionized species seen in UV/optical/IR);
(c) the gas kinematics inside the cavity, including the deviation (if any) from Keplerian rotation, the presence of radial flows (inflow/accretion streams or outflows/winds), and any non-axisymmetric components;
(d) the physical mechanism (or combination of mechanisms — e.g., dynamical clearing by one or more embedded planets, photoevaporative winds, magnetohydrodynamic disc winds, dead-zone dust trapping, grain growth) responsible for producing this structure;

and that quantitatively reconciles the apparent tension between (i) the inferred large drops in gas surface density inside the cavity and (ii) the simultaneous detections of gas (in molecular line emission, scattered-light tracers, and ongoing stellar accretion) within the same cavities.

Consider a protoplanetary (transition) disc of gas and dust orbiting a young pre-main-sequence star, in which one or more giant planets have opened an annular gap. The outer disc has a pressure maximum at the outer edge of the gap that traps millimetre-sized solids ("dust filtration"), producing a millimetre-bright ring with a resolved cavity at sub-/millimetre wavelengths. The system is also observed to:
(i) accrete gas onto the central star at rates comparable to those of full primordial discs (10^-9 to 10^-8 solar masses per year), so gas continues to flow through the gap;
(ii) show little or no near-infrared excess in its spectral energy distribution, indicating a strong depletion of warm small (1–10 micron) dust grains in the inner disc inside the cavity;
(iii) display scattered-light and thermal-infrared images (e.g., polarimetric near-infrared and mid-infrared) showing structure consistent with a depleted but not entirely empty cavity for small grains.

Aerodynamic theory shows that grains in the size range roughly 1–10 microns are well coupled to the gas: their Stokes number is small enough that they are dragged with the accretion flow through the gap and are not retained at the outer pressure bump by dust filtration alone. Hence, if dust filtration were the only process at work, micron-sized grains would populate the inner disc, dominate the opacity, and produce a strong near-infrared excess inconsistent with observation.

Problem. Identify and quantitatively justify a physical mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) that, in the presence of one or more gap-opening giant planets, can simultaneously:
(a) remove or sufficiently trap 1–10 micron dust grains from the inner disc to suppress the near-infrared excess;
(b) preserve a millimetre-bright outer ring with a sharply defined cavity at millimetre wavelengths;
(c) allow gas to continue accreting onto the star at observed rates;
(d) reproduce the structure seen in scattered-light and thermal-infrared imaging of transition discs.

State the mechanism, derive the governing equations describing the coupled gas–dust dynamics in the gap region (continuity, radial drift with drag, and any additional forces such as radiation pressure or photoevaporative wind), and demonstrate consistency with all four observational constraints above.

Let $V = \bigoplus_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} V_{(n)}$ be a simple vertex operator algebra such that:
(i) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$ and $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n < 0$;
(ii) the contragredient module $V'$ is isomorphic to $V$ as a $V$-module;
(iii) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e. $\dim V/C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V) = \mathrm{span}\{\mathrm{Res}_x x^{-2} Y(u,x)v : u,v \in V\}$;
(iv) every grading-restricted generalized $V$-module is completely reducible.

Let $G$ be a finite group of automorphisms of $V$. For each $g \in G$, consider the category of $g$-twisted (grading-restricted generalized) $V$-modules, and consider twisted intertwining operators of types $\binom{W_3}{W_1\, W_2}$ where $W_i$ is a $g_i$-twisted $V$-module ($g_i\in G$).

Question: Do these twisted intertwining operators (across all $g\in G$) satisfy the associativity property, the commutativity property, and the modular invariance property (the analogues, in the twisted/orbifold setting, of the corresponding properties known for untwisted intertwining operators among $V$-modules)?

Consider a Robertson–Walker braneworld cosmology obtained from a gravi-dilaton string effective action (or, equivalently, its Einstein-frame formulation), in which the matter content on the brane consists of a scalar field φ(t) with potential V(φ) coupled to a perfect fluid of energy density ρ and pressure p, with equation of state p = wρ. Assume the spatial sections are homogeneous and isotropic with scale factor a(t), and that the modified Friedmann equation on the brane has the schematic form H² = (8πG/3) ρ_tot (1 + ρ_tot/(2σ)) − k/a² + Λ/3, where σ is the brane tension and ρ_tot includes both the scalar-field energy density ρ_φ = ½φ̇² + V(φ) and the fluid energy density ρ. Suppose now that the scalar field and the fluid are allowed to exchange energy through a coupling term Q(φ,ρ,φ̇,H), so that the conservation equations read

  ρ̇_φ + 3H(ρ_φ + p_φ) = −Q,
  ρ̇ + 3H(ρ + p) = +Q.

Determine whether one can choose the potential V(φ), the equation-of-state parameter w, and the interaction term Q so that the resulting cosmological solution simultaneously:

 (i) is geodesically complete and free of curvature singularities for t → ±∞ (regular asymptotic behaviour at past and future infinity);

 (ii) satisfies the three constraints required of a physically admissible RW brane cosmology in the non-interacting case, namely:
   (a) the weak/strong/null energy conditions (or the appropriate brane energy condition) hold throughout the evolution;
   (b) the late-time evolution reproduces a standard cosmological epoch (e.g. an asymptotically de Sitter or radiation/matter-dominated phase consistent with observations);
   (c) the early-time behaviour avoids the initial Big Bang singularity, exhibiting either a bounce or an asymptotically flat past.

Either exhibit such an interacting model (V, w, Q) explicitly, or prove that no such choice exists.

A signed graph is a pair Σ = (G, σ) where G = (V, E) is a finite simple graph and σ : E → {+1, −1} is a sign function on its edges. The sign of a cycle (circle) in Σ is the product of the signs of its edges; a cycle is positive if its sign is +1 and negative if its sign is −1. The signed graph Σ is called balanced if every cycle in Σ is positive, and unbalanced otherwise. A Hamiltonian circle in Σ is a cycle in the underlying graph G that visits every vertex of V exactly once.

Suppose Σ is an unbalanced signed graph that contains at least one Hamiltonian circle. Determine, in general:

(a) whether Σ must contain a negative Hamiltonian circle;
(b) whether Σ must contain a positive Hamiltonian circle;
(c) whether Σ must contain both a positive and a negative Hamiltonian circle.

Provide either a proof of existence in each case or a counterexample (an unbalanced signed graph having a Hamiltonian circle but lacking the corresponding signed Hamiltonian circle).

Consider the two-dimensional diffraction problem associated with Young's double-slit experiment. Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be the screen consisting of the horizontal line $\{(x,0) : x \in \mathbb{R}\}$ with two finite open intervals (the slits) removed:
$$\Sigma = \{(x,0) : x \notin (a_1,b_1) \cup (a_2,b_2)\},$$
where $a_1<b_1<a_2<b_2$ are real numbers. Let $\Omega = \mathbb{R}^2 \setminus \Sigma$.

Given a wave number $k>0$, find an explicit closed-form expression for a function $u : \Omega \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfying:
1. The Helmholtz equation $\Delta u + k^2 u = 0$ in $\Omega$;
2. A Dirichlet (or, alternatively, Neumann) boundary condition $u = 0$ on $\Sigma$;
3. In the upper half-plane $\{y>0\}$, $u$ is the sum of an incident plane wave $u^{\mathrm{inc}}(x,y) = e^{-iky}$ and a scattered field $u^{\mathrm{sc}}$ that satisfies the Sommerfeld radiation condition
$$\lim_{r\to\infty} \sqrt{r}\left(\partial_r u^{\mathrm{sc}} - i k u^{\mathrm{sc}}\right) = 0$$
uniformly in angle, where $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$;
4. In the lower half-plane $\{y<0\}$, $u$ satisfies the Sommerfeld radiation condition;
5. Appropriate edge (Meixner) conditions at the four corners $(a_1,0), (b_1,0), (a_2,0), (b_2,0)$ ensuring uniqueness.

Provide a rigorous, mathematically explicit representation of the unique solution $u$ (for example, in terms of known special functions or convergent series) and use it to derive the diffraction pattern.

Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions at intermediate center-of-mass energies per nucleon pair (roughly a few GeV up to a few tens of GeV), where neither a purely viscous-hydrodynamic-plus-hadronic-afterburner hybrid description (appropriate at very high energies) nor a purely hadronic transport description (appropriate at very low energies) is uniformly valid throughout the collision volume.

Develop a single dynamical framework that smoothly interpolates between these two limits. Concretely, formulate a consistent space-time evolution model in which:

(i) regions of sufficiently high local energy density are treated by relativistic viscous hydrodynamics with a prescribed equation of state, while regions of lower density are treated by a microscopic hadronic transport (Boltzmann-type) description, with a well-defined dynamical interface (for example, a hydrodynamic core surrounded by a transport "corona", or localized hydrodynamic "bubbles" embedded inside a transport calculation, or an equivalent hybrid construction);

(ii) the matching between hydrodynamic cells and transport particles conserves energy, momentum, and conserved charges (baryon number, electric charge, strangeness) locally, and reduces to the standard high-energy hybrid model and to the standard hadronic transport model in the appropriate limits;

(iii) the equation of state used in the hydrodynamic regions incorporates a QCD phase transition and a critical end point, including non-equilibrium effects associated with critical slowing down, finite-size and finite-time effects, and fluctuations of the order parameter near the critical point.

The problem is to construct such a framework explicitly, prove (or demonstrate) that it provides a consistent interpolation between the two limiting descriptions, and specify how the phase transition and critical point — together with their non-equilibrium dynamics — are to be implemented within it.

Construct a single, fully coherent theoretical framework for heavy-quarkonium production in nucleus–nucleus collisions that simultaneously describes all available data on the J/ψ family (J/ψ, ψ(2S), χ_c) and the Υ family (Υ(1S), Υ(2S), Υ(3S), χ_b) across the SPS, RHIC, and LHC center-of-mass energies. The framework must consistently incorporate, within one calculation:

1. Lattice-QCD spectral functions for the in-medium quarkonium states (encoding screening and thermal widths as functions of temperature T);
2. Cold nuclear matter effects, including nuclear modifications of parton distribution functions (shadowing/antishadowing/EMC), nuclear absorption, and Cronin-type transverse-momentum broadening;
3. Parton energy loss in cold and hot nuclear matter;
4. Co-mover (hadronic and partonic comover) interactions;
5. Hydrodynamic evolution of the quark–gluon plasma background (with realistic initial conditions and equation of state);
6. Heavy-quark diffusion in the QGP, governed by a transport (drag/diffusion) coefficient, and the resulting regeneration of quarkonia from uncorrelated and correlated Q\bar{Q} pairs;
7. Feed-down contributions from higher excited states to the observed ground-state yields.

Within this framework, determine the nuclear modification factors R_AA (and elliptic flow v_2 where applicable) for each charmonium and bottomonium state as functions of collision energy √s_NN, centrality, transverse momentum, and rapidity, and answer the following specific question: does the sequential melting pattern of the Υ family, R_AA[Υ(1S)] > R_AA[Υ(2S)] > R_AA[Υ(3S)], change quantitatively between RHIC (√s_NN ≈ 200 GeV) and LHC (√s_NN ≈ 5 TeV) energies once all of the above ingredients are included, in a way that is consistent with measured data?

Consider a relativistic collision system that produces a small number of final-state particles, such as a proton–proton or proton–nucleus collision at LHC energies. Standard relativistic viscous hydrodynamics is expected to be valid only when (i) the ratio of the microscopic mean free path λ to the characteristic system size L satisfies λ/L ≪ 1, and (ii) the average number of secondary interactions experienced by a typical constituent during the lifetime of the system is large (≫ 1). Empirically, however, hydrodynamic simulations applied to such small and dilute systems reproduce observed azimuthal anisotropy coefficients v_n, identified-particle spectra, and other collective observables to a degree comparable with their success in large nucleus–nucleus collisions, even though the conditions (i)–(ii) are apparently not satisfied.

Provide a quantitative dynamical explanation, derived from an underlying microscopic theory of QCD or from a controlled effective description, that accounts for why a hydrodynamic (or hydrodynamic-like) description yields the observed collective behavior in these small dilute systems. The explanation should specify:
(a) the mechanism by which approximate local equilibration or "hydrodynamization" is reached on the relevant short timescales, if any equilibration is achieved at all;
(b) the regime of validity of the effective hydrodynamic description in terms of λ/L, opacity, or analogous parameters;
(c) the matching between the pre-equilibrium dynamics and the hydrodynamic stage in systems with very few scatterings per particle;
(d) testable predictions distinguishing genuine hydrodynamic flow from alternative origins (e.g., initial-state momentum correlations of color-glass-condensate type or kinetic-theory few-scattering effects).

Let A be a finite alphabet with |A| ≥ 2 and let A* denote the set of finite words over A. A pattern is a word p over an alphabet of variables E = {x_1, x_2, ...}. A word w ∈ A* is said to encounter the pattern p if there exists a non-erasing morphism h : E* → A* such that h(p) occurs as a factor of w; otherwise w avoids p. The pattern p is k-avoidable if there exists an infinite word over a k-letter alphabet that avoids p, and avoidable if it is k-avoidable for some k; the avoidability index λ(p) is the least such k (and ∞ if p is unavoidable).

Develop and apply probabilistic-method techniques (such as the Lovász Local Lemma, entropy compression, the cluster-expansion / Moser–Tardos framework, or related random constructions) to obtain new results in pattern avoidance for words. Concretely, formulate and prove probabilistic-method theorems that yield improved bounds on quantities such as:

(a) the avoidability index λ(p) of a given avoidable pattern p, or general upper bounds on λ(p) in terms of structural parameters of p (e.g., length, number of distinct variables, or maximum exponent);

(b) the growth rate (entropy) of the language of words over a k-letter alphabet avoiding a given pattern or set of patterns, in particular sharper lower bounds showing the language is exponentially large;

(c) thresholds for avoidability of fractional powers, abelian patterns, k-abelian patterns, or formulas, including determination or improved estimates of the repetition threshold for new classes of patterns.

Provide a unified probabilistic framework for pattern avoidance over words and use it to settle (or quantitatively advance) at least one previously open instance among (a)–(c).

Let $\Sigma$ be a finite alphabet and let $k, n \geq 2$ be integers. Two finite words $u, v \in \Sigma^*$ are said to be strongly $k$-abelian equivalent, written $u \sim_k v$, if either $|u|, |v| < k$ and $u = v$, or $|u|, |v| \geq k$ and the following hold:
(i) $u$ and $v$ have the same prefix of length $k-1$;
(ii) $u$ and $v$ have the same suffix of length $k-1$;
(iii) for every word $f \in \Sigma^k$, the number of occurrences of $f$ as a factor of $u$ equals the number of occurrences of $f$ as a factor of $v$.

A word $w \in \Sigma^*$ is called a strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th power if $w$ can be written as a concatenation $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_n$ of nonempty words such that $w_i \sim_k w_j$ for all $1 \leq i, j \leq n$. A word $u \in \Sigma^*$ is said to avoid strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th powers if no factor of $u$ is a strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th power.

For each finite alphabet $\Sigma$ and each pair of integers $k, n \geq 2$, determine the supremum (and, when finite, the exact value) of the lengths of finite words over $\Sigma$ that avoid strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th powers.

Let $A$ be a finite alphabet and $A^*$ the free monoid of finite words over $A$. Given a family $F \subseteq A^*$ of nonempty words, an $F$-factorization of a word $w \in A^*$ is a factorization $w = u_1 u_2 \cdots u_k$ with each $u_i \in F$. The factorization is called regular when $F$ is a regular language, and we call it a finite-language $F$-factorization when $F$ is a finite language.

Consider the standard algorithmic problems associated with $F$-factorizations, such as:
(i) deciding, given $w \in A^*$, whether $w$ admits an $F$-factorization;
(ii) counting the number of distinct $F$-factorizations of $w$;
(iii) producing one (or all) $F$-factorization(s) of $w$;
(iv) computing an optimal $F$-factorization with respect to a natural cost (e.g.\ minimum or maximum number of factors).

The benchmark complexities are those obtained when $F$ is allowed to be an arbitrary regular language, given by a finite automaton or regular expression of size $m$, with input word length $n$.

Question: Can asymptotically faster algorithms (in $n$ and in the size of the description of $F$) be designed for problems (i)–(iv) under the restriction that $F$ is a finite language, compared with the best known algorithms in the general regular case?

Consider a family of polynomials $\widetilde{W}_n(z^2;\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi)$, indexed by $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ and depending on parameters $\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi$, that arises as a deformation of the Wilson polynomial family and satisfies a deformed three-term recursion relation (the modified Wilson polynomials, also referred to in the literature as Racah–Heun polynomials). For this family, determine the following analytic properties and, where possible, an explicit closed-form expression:

(a) a positive weight function (or measure) $w(z;\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi)$ with respect to which the polynomials are orthogonal, together with the corresponding orthogonality relation;
(b) a generating function $\sum_{n\ge 0} \widetilde{W}_n(z^2;\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi)\, t^n / c_n$ in closed form;
(c) the asymptotic behavior of $\widetilde{W}_n(z^2;\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi)$ as $n\to\infty$ and as $z\to\infty$;
(d) a Rodrigues-type formula expressing $\widetilde{W}_n$ via repeated application of a differential or difference operator to a base function;
(e) explicit forward and backward shift operator relations linking $\widetilde{W}_n$ to $\widetilde{W}_{n\pm 1}$ with shifted parameters;
(f) the location and qualitative behavior of the zeros of $\widetilde{W}_n$;
(g) where possible, an explicit closed-form representation of $\widetilde{W}_n(z^2;\kappa,\tau,\eta,\xi)$ (for instance, in terms of (basic) hypergeometric series or known special functions).

In general relativity, an evolving black hole's boundary is intuitively a hypersurface in spacetime that grows as matter and radiation fall in. The classical notion of an event horizon is global and teleological, requiring knowledge of the entire future of spacetime, and is therefore unsuitable for many local and dynamical considerations. Several quasi-local proposals exist, including trapping horizons, dynamical horizons (smooth spacelike hypersurfaces foliated by marginally trapped 2-surfaces on which one null expansion vanishes and the other is strictly negative), future outer trapping horizons, and various marginally outer trapped tube constructions. However, these notions are not unique: the foliation by marginally trapped surfaces can fail to be unique, the hypersurface need not be everywhere spacelike under generic dynamics, jumps and bifurcations can occur, and existence/regularity in nonsymmetric spacetimes is not fully understood.

Problem. Formulate a mathematically precise definition of a "dynamical horizon" — a quasi-local hypersurface representing the moving boundary of an evolving black hole in a 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime (M, g) satisfying the Einstein equations with physically reasonable matter — that simultaneously:
(i) is purely quasi-local (depends only on a neighborhood of the hypersurface, not on global causal structure or asymptotic flatness);
(ii) reduces to a Killing/isolated horizon in equilibrium;
(iii) admits a unique, canonical foliation by closed marginally trapped 2-surfaces (so that area, angular momentum, and flux laws are unambiguously defined);
(iv) exists and is sufficiently regular for generic dynamical evolutions (e.g., gravitational collapse and binary mergers), with controlled behavior under bifurcations and jumps;
(v) yields a consistent first law and flux formulae for energy and angular momentum carried across the horizon.
Exhibit such a definition and prove its existence, uniqueness (up to a specified equivalence), and regularity, or prove that no definition meeting all of (i)–(v) exists.

Let $(M,g)$ be a four-dimensional smooth Lorentzian manifold serving as a spacetime in general relativity, with metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ satisfying the Einstein field equations $G_{\mu\nu}[g] = 8\pi G\, T_{\mu\nu}$, where $G_{\mu\nu}[g]$ is the Einstein tensor and $T_{\mu\nu}$ is the energy–momentum tensor of matter fields defined on $M$.

Provide a mathematically rigorous, generally covariant definition of an averaging (coarse-graining) operation that takes as input the geometric data $g_{\mu\nu}$ and the matter data $T_{\mu\nu}$ on $M$ at a "fine" scale and produces, on the same differentiable manifold (or a quotient/refinement thereof), coarse-grained tensor fields $\langle g_{\mu\nu}\rangle$ and $\langle T_{\mu\nu}\rangle$ such that:

(i) the averaging procedure is independent of the choice of coordinates and respects the tensorial/covariant character of the fields being averaged (in particular, the average of a tensor field is a well-defined tensor field);
(ii) the procedure is defined on a generic differentiable manifold without requiring auxiliary background structure (such as a preferred foliation, Killing symmetries, or a fixed background metric) beyond what can be canonically constructed;
(iii) it specifies how the nonlinear Einstein equations transform under the averaging, yielding a well-posed system of equations governing the coarse-grained fields, with explicit "backreaction" or correlation terms accounting for the failure of averaging to commute with the Einstein operator;
(iv) in appropriate limits (e.g. high-symmetry or near-homogeneous regimes) it reduces to the expected naive averages.

Construct such a framework, or prove that no construction satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

Consider a quantum field theory coupled to gravity in which a black hole forms from the gravitational collapse of matter initially prepared in a pure quantum state, and subsequently evaporates completely via Hawking radiation. Semiclassical analysis (treating the quantum fields on a fixed classical curved spacetime background) predicts that the asymptotic outgoing radiation is described by a thermal density matrix, so that the final state is mixed even though the initial state was pure. This is in tension with the unitary evolution required by quantum mechanics, which preserves the purity of states and forbids genuine loss of information.

Problem: Provide a mathematically and physically consistent description of black-hole formation and complete evaporation that:
(i) reproduces the predictions of semiclassical general relativity and quantum field theory in curved spacetime in their respective regimes of validity (in particular, the approximately thermal Hawking spectrum at temperature T = ℏ c^3 /(8π G M k_B) for a Schwarzschild black hole of mass M);
(ii) is compatible with unitary quantum-mechanical evolution from the initial pure state of collapsing matter to the final state of outgoing radiation (and any remnants), so that no information is genuinely lost;
(iii) specifies the mechanism by which the information initially encoded in the collapsing matter is recovered in, or correlated with, the outgoing radiation, and identifies where and how the semiclassical derivation of thermal radiation breaks down.

In short: formulate and justify a resolution of the apparent conflict between Hawking's semiclassical prediction of thermal black-hole radiation and the unitarity of quantum mechanics.

Construct a mathematically rigorous and physically consistent theory of quantum gravity. Concretely, give a well-defined mathematical framework (for instance a Hilbert space of states, a set of observables forming a suitable algebra, and a dynamical law) that:

(a) reduces, in an appropriate classical/low-energy limit, to Einstein's general relativity, in which spacetime is modeled by a smooth Lorentzian 4-manifold (M, g) whose metric g satisfies the Einstein field equations
   R_{μν} − (1/2) R g_{μν} + Λ g_{μν} = (8πG/c^4) T_{μν};

(b) reduces, in an appropriate flat-spacetime or weak-gravity limit, to standard quantum mechanics / quantum field theory, including unitary time evolution and the usual probabilistic interpretation of measurements;

(c) is internally mathematically coherent (free of the non-renormalizable ultraviolet divergences and other inconsistencies that plague the naive perturbative quantization of general relativity) in the regime where both quantum effects and strong gravitational effects are simultaneously relevant (e.g. near black-hole singularities and at the Planck scale);

(d) provides definite, in-principle computable predictions for physical quantities in this regime.

The problem is to either exhibit such a theory together with a proof of its consistency, or to prove that no such theory can exist within a specified set of axioms.

Let $p$ be a prime number and let $\mathbb{Q}_p$ denote the field of $p$-adic numbers, equipped with the $p$-adic valuation $v_p$. Consider the following two infinite totally ramified algebraic extensions of $\mathbb{Q}_p$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $K_1 = \mathbb{Q}_p(\zeta_{p^\infty})$, obtained by adjoining a compatible system of primitive $p^n$-th roots of unity $\zeta_{p^n}$ for all $n \geq 1$;
\item $K_2 = \mathbb{Q}_p(p^{1/p^\infty})$, obtained by adjoining a compatible system of $p^n$-th roots of $p$ for all $n \geq 1$.
\end{itemize}
Each $K_i$ is regarded as a valued field with the unique extension of $v_p$. Recall that a valued field $(K,v)$ is called Kaplansky (i.e., satisfies Kaplansky's hypothesis (A)) if its residue characteristic $p$ satisfies: the value group is $p$-divisible, the residue field is perfect, and the residue field admits no finite separable extension whose degree is divisible by $p$. Recall further that a maximal immediate extension of $(K,v)$ is an extension with the same value group and residue field that admits no proper immediate extension, and that a henselian valued field of residue characteristic $p>0$ is called tame if it is algebraically maximal, its value group is $p$-divisible, and its residue field is perfect.

Neither $K_1$ nor $K_2$ is a Kaplansky field, and consequently each admits uncountably many pairwise non-isomorphic maximal immediate extensions, realising uncountably many distinct elementary theories in the language $L_{\mathrm{val}}$ of valued fields, of which uncountably many are undecidable. Among these maximal immediate extensions, those whose elementary $L_{\mathrm{val}}$-theory is decidable are known to be tame valued fields.

Problem. Describe the internal valuation-theoretic and algebraic structure of the maximal immediate extensions of $(K_i, v_p)$ for $i = 1, 2$, beyond the mere cardinality statement that there are infinitely (in fact uncountably) many. In particular, identify intrinsic structural invariants or properties of such a maximal immediate extension that characterise when its elementary $L_{\mathrm{val}}$-theory is decidable versus undecidable.

Consider a solar flare modeled as a magnetized plasma system in which magnetic reconnection releases stored magnetic energy and produces nonthermal populations of energetic electrons (with energies in the deka- to hecto-keV range) and energetic ions (with energies ranging from about 0.01 to 100 MeV). Several candidate acceleration mechanisms have been proposed:

(a) merging of magnetic islands/plasmoids inside reconnecting current sheets;
(b) acceleration at fast-mode termination shocks formed where reconnection outflows impinge on closed flare loops;
(c) direct (DC) parallel electric fields in the reconnection region;
(d) stochastic/turbulent acceleration, including acceleration by turbulence generated within fragmented current sheets;
(e) local acceleration in the chromosphere.

Address the following questions:

1. Identify the dominant mechanism(s) in the list above responsible for accelerating electrons and ions in solar flares, and determine the spatial location(s) within the flare geometry where the bulk of the acceleration occurs.
2. Quantify how the relative contributions of these mechanisms vary from flare to flare and within the temporal evolution of a single flare.
3. Determine which physical parameters of a flare control its efficiency as a particle accelerator, measured both by the total number of accelerated particles and by the maximum energy attained in the resulting distributions.
4. Determine the pitch-angle anisotropy of the accelerated electron and ion distributions.
5. Determine whether electrons and protons are accelerated by a common process (co-spatial and co-temporal) or by separate mechanisms, and characterize the relationship between their spectra, numbers, and timing.
6. Determine the ratio of the total energy carried by energetic ions to that carried by energetic electrons, and the partition of energy between these populations as a function of flare properties.

Let $p$ be a prime number. Consider the following valued fields, each equipped with its natural valuation:

(a) $\mathbb{Q}_p(\zeta_{p^\infty})$, the field obtained from the $p$-adic numbers $\mathbb{Q}_p$ by adjoining all $p$-power roots of unity;
(b) $\mathbb{Q}_p(p^{1/p^\infty})$, obtained from $\mathbb{Q}_p$ by adjoining a compatible system of $p$-power roots of $p$;
(c) $\mathbb{Q}_p^{\mathrm{ab}}$, the maximal abelian extension of $\mathbb{Q}_p$;
(d) $\mathbb{F}_p((t))^{1/p^\infty}$, the perfect hull of the formal Laurent series field $\mathbb{F}_p((t))$;
(e) $\mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{ac}}((t))^{1/p^\infty}$, the perfect hull of $\mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{ac}}((t))$, where $\mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{ac}}$ is an algebraic closure of $\mathbb{F}_p$.

Let $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}}$ be the language of valued rings (the ring language together with a binary predicate or unary divisibility relation expressing the valuation), and let $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}}(t) = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}} \cup \{t\}$ be its expansion by a constant symbol naming the uniformizer $t$.

Determine whether the complete theories of the structures in (a), (b), and (c) in the language $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}}$ are decidable. Determine whether the complete theories of the structures in (d) and (e) are decidable in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}}$, and whether they are decidable in the richer language $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{val}}(t)$.

Wolf-Rayet (WR) stars are evolved, hydrogen-depleted massive stars exhibiting strong, broad emission-line spectra driven by powerful stellar winds; they are subdivided into nitrogen-sequence (WN) and carbon-sequence (WC) classes with ionization-based subtypes (e.g., WN5–7, WC8–9). Standard single-star evolutionary scenarios produce WR stars either through intrinsic radiation- and metallicity-driven mass loss in the very massive star (VMS) regime, or through envelope removal during a luminous blue variable (LBV) phase including giant eruptions; alternatively, in binary systems, Roche-lobe overflow or other forms of binary interaction can strip the hydrogen envelope, possibly together with brief intermediate stripping phases.

Consider the observed Galactic and Local Group WR population, with Westerlund 1 (Wd1) — whose WR census is dominated by WN5–7 and WC8–9 subtypes that show infrared dust excesses and X-ray luminosities indicative of binarity — taken as a representative test case of lower-luminosity WR stars with strong winds.

Determine the evolutionary pathways that produce the bulk of this observed WR population, in particular the lower-luminosity strong-wind subtypes such as WN5–7 and WC8–9. Quantify the relative contributions of:
(i) intrinsic radiation-driven mass loss in single stars;
(ii) envelope loss through LBV-type winds and giant eruptions in single-star evolution;
(iii) binary interaction (mass transfer, common-envelope evolution, stripping by a companion);
(iv) short-lived intermediate stripping phases;
and reconcile the resulting predicted current structures, luminosities, surface compositions, and binary fractions with the observed WR populations whose configurations or formation routes are not accounted for by standard single-star evolutionary predictions.

Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n 	imes n}$ be a symmetric positive definite matrix and let $b \in \mathbb{R}^n$. Consider solving the linear system $Ax = b$ by two classical Krylov subspace methods:

1. The Conjugate Gradient (CG) method (in a standard, numerically sound implementation, e.g. the Hestenes–Stiefel three-term recurrence with the usual choice of $\alpha_k = (r_k^	op r_k)/(p_k^	op A p_k)$ and $ eta_k = (r_{k+1}^	op r_{k+1})/(r_k^	op r_k)$), which produces iterates $x_k$ minimizing the $A$-norm of the error over $x_0 + \mathcal{K}_k(A,r_0)$, where $\mathcal{K}_k(A,r_0) = \mathrm{span}\{r_0, A r_0, \dots, A^{k-1} r_0\}$.

2. The symmetric Lanczos method (in a standard implementation, e.g. with the three-term recurrence $A q_k =  eta_{k-1} q_{k-1} + \alpha_k q_k +  eta_k q_{k+1}$ and no reorthogonalization), used to build an orthonormal basis of $\mathcal{K}_k(A,r_0)$ and a tridiagonal matrix $T_k$, from which an approximate solution $x_k^{\mathrm{Lan}} = x_0 + Q_k T_k^{-1}(\|r_0\|\, e_1)$ is recovered.

In exact arithmetic these two methods produce mathematically identical iterates $x_k$. However, when carried out in finite precision floating-point arithmetic with unit roundoff $u$, both methods suffer loss of orthogonality among the computed Krylov vectors and delay of convergence.

Question: Determine whether these two algorithms behave similarly in finite precision arithmetic when applied to symmetric positive definite systems. Specifically, compare and analyze in finite precision:

(a) the loss of orthogonality among the computed residuals (CG) versus the computed Lanczos vectors;
(b) the rate at which the $A$-norm of the error and the $2$-norm of the residual decrease as functions of the iteration index $k$;
(c) the maximum attainable accuracy (final level at which the residual stagnates);
(d) the appearance of "ghost" or duplicated Ritz values approximating eigenvalues of $A$ in the tridiagonal matrices produced by the two methods.

Provide either a proof that, with these standard implementations, the iterates and convergence behavior of CG and Lanczos remain essentially equivalent in finite precision (giving quantitative bounds in terms of $u$, $n$, and the spectrum of $A$), or exhibit families of symmetric positive definite systems on which the two methods diverge meaningfully in their finite-precision behavior, with an explanation of the mechanism.

Let T be an n×n real symmetric tridiagonal matrix, and let a subset of its eigenpairs be desired. The Algorithm of Multiple Relatively Robust Representations (MR^3) computes approximate eigenpairs of T as follows. It maintains a representation tree whose nodes are bidiagonal factorizations LDL^T of shifted matrices T - σI. At the root, an initial relatively robust representation L_0 D_0 L_0^T = T - σ_0 I is formed. Approximate eigenvalues are computed for each representation; eigenvalues are partitioned into singletons (relatively isolated) and clusters (close in a relative sense). For each singleton λ, an approximate eigenvector is computed by a twisted-factorization / RQI-type step from the local representation. For each cluster, a new shift σ' near the cluster is chosen, a new factorization L'D'(L')^T = LDL^T - σ' I is formed via differential stationary qd transforms (dstqds), and the algorithm recurses. All arithmetic is performed in finite-precision floating-point with unit roundoff ε.

Provide a "TCS-style" worst-case analysis of MR^3: that is, exhibit a finite list of conditions on the input matrix T (and on user-specified parameters such as the cluster-separation threshold, the relative-gap tolerance, and the shift selection rule) that
(i) can each be checked, given T and the parameters, in time polynomial in n and in the bit length of the input;
(ii) imply that the algorithm terminates in a number of arithmetic operations bounded by an explicit polynomial in n and the number k of requested eigenpairs;
(iii) imply that the computed eigenvectors v̂_1, …, v̂_k satisfy explicit a priori bounds of the form ‖T v̂_i − λ̂_i v̂_i‖ ≤ p(n) ε ‖T‖ and |v̂_i^T v̂_j| ≤ q(n) ε for i ≠ j, with p and q explicit polynomials.

Equivalently, formulate verifiable sufficient conditions on T alone (e.g., on element growth, relative gaps, and pivot magnitudes encountered in the canonical execution) under which MR^3 is provably correct and convergent, and prove the resulting orthogonality and residual bounds.

Pulsars are rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized neutron stars observed as periodic sources of electromagnetic radiation, and they are known to interact with their surroundings through relativistic winds, high-energy emission, supernova-remnant injection, and gravitational interactions with companion stars. Globular clusters are dense, gravitationally bound stellar systems in which frequent stellar encounters, exchange interactions, and tidal captures produce a high incidence of binary systems and recycled (millisecond) pulsars; the Galactic field, by contrast, consists of comparatively low-density stellar populations in which neutron stars evolve under largely isolated conditions following their progenitor supernovae.

Characterize quantitatively and qualitatively the role that pulsars play in cosmic evolution and in stellar dynamics, addressing in particular:

(1) the channels through which pulsars contribute to the energetic, chemical, and dynamical evolution of their host environments (e.g., feedback from pulsar winds and relativistic outflows, contributions to cosmic-ray populations, heating of the interstellar/intracluster medium, and gravitational influence on neighboring stars);

(2) how these contributions differ between (a) the dense, interaction-rich environment of globular clusters, where dynamical formation channels (tidal capture, exchange encounters, three-body interactions) shape the pulsar binary population and recycling history, and (b) the Galactic field, where pulsar populations are dominated by primordial binary evolution and isolated spin-down;

(3) the resulting differences in observable population statistics, such as the relative abundance of millisecond versus normal pulsars, binary companion distributions, spatial and kinematic distributions, and spin/magnetic-field evolution, as a function of environment.

Provide a unified description that links the local micro-physics of pulsar emission and binary interaction to the macroscopic evolution of the host stellar system.

A finite-dimensional billiard system consists of geodesic motion on a Riemannian manifold with boundary, with elastic reflection (equal angles of incidence and reflection) along the boundary. It is classical that billiard flows arise as limits of geodesic flows on manifolds where a tubular neighborhood of a hypersurface is collapsed, so billiards and geodesic flows are closely related.

In infinite dimensions, many equations of mathematical physics are realized as geodesic equations of right-invariant metrics on infinite-dimensional Lie groups (Euler–Arnold equations). For example:
 (i) the Euler equation of an ideal incompressible fluid is the geodesic equation of the right-invariant L^2 metric on the group of volume-preserving diffeomorphisms of a Riemannian manifold;
 (ii) the KdV equation arises as a geodesic equation on the Virasoro group with a right-invariant L^2 metric;
 (iii) similar geodesic interpretations exist for the equations of magnetohydrodynamics, the Heisenberg magnetic chain, and other PDEs.

Problem. Construct physically meaningful examples of infinite-dimensional billiard systems, that is, geodesic flows with elastic reflection along a codimension-one "wall" (a smooth hypersurface, or a suitable subset on which a reflection law can be defined) in an infinite-dimensional Riemannian manifold or Lie group equipped with a right-invariant metric, such that the resulting reflecting geodesic dynamics corresponds to (or arises naturally from) an equation of mathematical physics — for instance a constrained or reflected variant of the Euler equation of hydrodynamics, KdV, magnetohydrodynamics, or another Euler–Arnold geodesic equation. Specify the underlying group/manifold, the right-invariant metric, the reflecting hypersurface, the reflection law, and the associated PDE.

Provide a precise, operational definition of a "planetary nebula" that satisfies both of the following requirements simultaneously:

(1) Physical/evolutionary requirement. The definition must require that the object consist of an ionized circumstellar shell of gas and dust whose progenitor was a star evolving off the asymptotic giant branch (AGB), and whose ionizing source is a hot post-AGB star or pre-white-dwarf central star produced by that progenitor.

(2) Discriminatory requirement. The definition must, when applied to observational data (imaging, emission-line spectroscopy, kinematics, photometry, central-star characterization, etc.), reliably exclude each of the following classes of "mimics" that can resemble planetary nebulae in one or more diagnostics:
   (a) emission-line galaxies (e.g., compact H II galaxies, AGN narrow-line regions);
   (b) reflection nebulae;
   (c) H II regions ionized by massive young stars;
   (d) Wolf–Rayet ring nebulae and other massive-star ejecta;
   (e) classical and recurrent novae and their shells;
   (f) supernova remnants;
   (g) young stellar objects and their associated nebulosity (e.g., Herbig–Haro flows, proto-stellar shells);
   (h) symbiotic stars and their ionized circumstellar material.

State the definition as a finite list of necessary and sufficient observational and physical criteria (for example, conditions on the mass and evolutionary state of the central star, on the chemical and kinematic properties of the shell, and on energetics and morphology) such that an object qualifies as a planetary nebula if and only if it satisfies all listed criteria, and prove (or otherwise justify) that the criteria correctly include bona-fide planetary nebulae while excluding every class of mimic listed in (2).

A planetary nebula is the ionized circumstellar shell ejected by a low- to intermediate-mass star (initial mass roughly 1–8 solar masses) as it transitions from the asymptotic giant branch (AGB) to the white dwarf stage. Its evolution traditionally consists of: (i) a slow, dense, approximately spherical AGB wind (terminal speed ~10–20 km/s, mass-loss rates up to ~10^{-4} solar masses per year during the superwind); (ii) a proto-planetary-nebula (pre-PN) phase, during which the central star contracts and heats while the AGB envelope detaches; (iii) the planetary-nebula phase proper, when a fast (~10^3 km/s) tenuous post-AGB wind from the hot central star drives a shock into the previously ejected slow wind, and ionizing UV radiation lights up the nebula. Many observed planetary nebulae display non-spherical morphologies classified as bipolar (two opposed lobes separated by an equatorial waist), multipolar (multiple pairs of lobes), or point-symmetric (features symmetric under inversion through the central star).

Identify the dominant physical mechanism that first imprints departures from spherical symmetry on the circumstellar matter that later evolves into bipolar, multipolar, or point-symmetric planetary nebulae, and determine at which of the following evolutionary stages such asymmetries first emerge:
(a) already during the AGB superwind itself;
(b) during the proto-planetary-nebula phase, before the fast central-star wind switches on;
(c) only after the onset of the fast post-AGB wind interacting with the slow AGB wind via the generalized interacting-stellar-winds mechanism.

Candidate mechanisms to be evaluated include, but are not restricted to: rapid stellar rotation, large-scale magnetic fields anchored in the AGB envelope, single-star pulsation/convection-driven asymmetric mass loss, common-envelope evolution with a stellar or substellar companion, wind Roche-lobe overflow in a wide binary, accretion onto a companion launching collimated jets, and disk-mediated magnetohydrodynamic outflows. Provide the determination of the responsible process and the corresponding onset epoch.

Consider the class of singular nonlinear elliptic boundary value problems of the form
\[
-\Delta u + g(u) = \lambda f(x,u) \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u \ge 0 \text{ in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega,
\]
where \(\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N\) (\(N>1\)) is a bounded smooth domain, \(\lambda>0\) is a parameter, \(f\) is a nonnegative source, and \(g:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)\) is a strong absorption term that is singular or non-Lipschitz at \(0\) (a typical model is \(g(u)=u^q\) with \(0<q<1\), possibly with \(g(0)=0\) and \(g'(0^+)=+\infty\)). For such problems, "free boundary" solutions are nonnegative weak solutions whose support \(\{u>0\}\) is strictly contained in \(\Omega\), so that the boundary of the support inside \(\Omega\) is a free boundary; "flat solutions" are those satisfying both \(u=0\) and \(\partial_\nu u=0\) on \(\partial\Omega\). Assume that, for the Laplacian diffusion above, multiplicity results are known: there exist parameter ranges of \(\lambda\) for which the problem admits at least two distinct positive solutions, and/or distinct free boundary solutions, and/or coexistence of a positive solution with a compactly supported (free boundary) solution.

The problem is to determine which of these multiplicity phenomena persist when the diffusion operator \(-\Delta\) is replaced by each of the following, while keeping the same type of singular absorption \(g(u)\) and source \(\lambda f(x,u)\):

(a) a general linear second-order uniformly elliptic operator \(L u = -\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}(x)\partial_{ij} u + \sum_i b_i(x)\partial_i u + c(x) u\) with smooth coefficients;

(b) the \(p\)-Laplacian \(-\Delta_p u = -\operatorname{div}(|\nabla u|^{p-2}\nabla u)\) for \(1<p<\infty\);

(c) fully nonlinear operators of Hamilton–Jacobi type, e.g. \(-\Delta u + H(x,\nabla u)\) with \(H\) of suitable growth in the gradient;

(d) the Monge–Ampère operator \(\det(D^2 u)\) (in the appropriate convex/concave setting);

(e) the bi-Laplacian \(\Delta^2\) with appropriate (Dirichlet or Navier) boundary conditions;

(f) cooperative or competitive nonlinear elliptic systems and Schrödinger systems with singular absorption acting componentwise.

For each of (a)–(f), establish (or disprove) the existence of parameter regimes producing at least two distinct nonnegative solutions, and characterize when free boundary / flat solutions arise alongside strictly positive solutions, in analogy with the Laplacian case.

Let $M$ be a smooth manifold. A Lorentzian metric on $M$ is a smooth, non-degenerate symmetric $(0,2)$-tensor field of signature $(-,+,\ldots,+)$, while a Riemannian metric on $M$ is a smooth, positive-definite symmetric $(0,2)$-tensor field. The classical Wick rotation is the procedure of analytically continuing the time coordinate $t \mapsto i\tau$ in a static Lorentzian metric of the form $g = -dt^2 + h$ (with $h$ a Riemannian metric on a spatial slice) so as to obtain the Riemannian metric $g_E = d\tau^2 + h$.

Formulate and prove a generalization of the Wick rotation that applies beyond the static case. Specifically, identify intrinsic geometric, topological, and/or analytic conditions on a Lorentzian manifold $(M,g_L)$—possibly involving the existence of a distinguished timelike vector field, a foliation by spacelike hypersurfaces, an analytic structure, curvature constraints, or an embedding into a complexification—under which there exists a canonical procedure producing a Riemannian metric $g_R$ on $M$ (or on a related manifold) such that:
(i) the construction reduces to the standard Wick rotation in the static case;
(ii) the correspondence $g_L \leftrightarrow g_R$ is well-defined and, in an appropriate sense, natural (for instance, compatible with diffeomorphisms and with analytic continuation of curvature invariants);
(iii) the conditions are sharp, in that a Lorentzian metric admits such a "Wick rotation" to a Riemannian metric if and only if the stated conditions hold.

Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on $(M,g_L)$ for the existence of such a generalized Wick rotation.

Let $\Sigma$ be a finite alphabet and $\Sigma^*$ the set of finite words over $\Sigma$. For a word $w \in \Sigma^*$, define the prefix-suffix square completion relation $\Rightarrow$ on $\Sigma^*$ as follows:
- (suffix square completion) $w \Rightarrow wx$ whenever $w$ has a suffix of the form $yx$ with $|x| \ge 1$ (so that $wx$ ends with the square $yxyx$ — i.e., $w = uyx$ and the new word $wx = uyxyx$);
- (prefix square completion) $w \Rightarrow xw$ whenever $w$ has a prefix of the form $xy$ with $|x| \ge 1$ (so that $xw$ starts with the square $xyxy$).

Let $\Rightarrow^*$ be the reflexive-transitive closure of $\Rightarrow$. For a language $L \subseteq \Sigma^*$, define
$$\mathrm{PSC}^*(L) = \{ v \in \Sigma^* : \exists\, w \in L,\ w \Rightarrow^* v \}.$$

Carry out a systematic study of the languages $\mathrm{PSC}^*(L)$ obtained by iterated prefix-suffix square completion when the initial set $L$ ranges over distinguished families of languages, in particular:
(i) singleton sets $L = \{w\}$;
(ii) arbitrary finite sets;
(iii) regular languages.

Investigate structural properties such as membership of $\mathrm{PSC}^*(L)$ in the Chomsky hierarchy (regular, context-free, context-sensitive), closure properties, decidability of membership, growth, and the structure of the resulting languages in each case.
